var SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "architecture.html", "title": "Architecture Overview", "subtitle": "System design, the two backends, communication patterns, threading model, startup order,\n            observability, and the reasoning behind each of those choices \u2014 verified against\n            src/main.cpp, main.py and the manager sources.", "headings": [{"id": "high-level", "text": "High-Level Architecture", "level": 2}, {"id": "backend-frontend", "text": "Backend / Frontend Split", "level": 2}, {"id": "manager-pattern", "text": "The Manager Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "all-managers", "text": "All Managers (both backends)", "level": 3}, {"id": "communication", "text": "Backend-QML Communication", "level": 2}, {"id": "context-properties", "text": "Context Properties", "level": 3}, {"id": "signals-slots", "text": "Signals and Slots", "level": 3}, {"id": "properties", "text": "Qt Properties", "level": 3}, {"id": "qml-types", "text": "Custom QML Types", "level": 3}, {"id": "startup", "text": "Application Startup Sequence", "level": 2}, {"id": "startup-cpp", "text": "C++ &mdash; src/main.cpp", "level": 3}, {"id": "startup-python", "text": "Python &mdash; main.py", "level": 3}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "thread-obd", "text": "OBD Worker Thread", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-pool", "text": "Thread Pools and QtConcurrent", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-sensors", "text": "Sensor Read Loops", "level": 3}, {"id": "thread-watchdog", "text": "UI Watchdog", "level": 3}, {"id": "volume-system", "text": "Volume System", "level": 2}, {"id": "video", "text": "Video into QML: Image Provider vs QVideoSink", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-server", "text": "The Phone Server Fork", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-persistence", "text": "Settings Persistence", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboards", "text": "Dashboards: JSON Specs + DashboardManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "vehicle-3d", "text": "The 3D Vehicle (CarMenu)", "level": 2}, {"id": "logging", "text": "Logging, Crash Handlers and Diagnostics", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-tooling", "text": "Profiling and the Command Server", "level": 2}, {"id": "signal-wiring", "text": "Signal Wiring Graph", "level": 2}, {"id": "cleanup", "text": "Shutdown &amp; Cleanup", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions &mdash; Why It Is Built This Way", "level": 2}], "body": "Architecture - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Architecture Architecture Overview System design, the two backends, communication patterns, threading model, startup order, observability, and the reasoning behind each of those choices \u2014 verified against src/main.cpp , main.py and the manager sources. High-Level Architecture OCTAVE is a backend/frontend split joined by Qt's signals and slots. There are two parallel backends that expose the same API surface to one shared QML frontend: a C++ / Qt 6 backend in src/ \u2014 the only binary that ships on every platform (Windows .exe , macOS .dmg , Linux x86_64 and aarch64 AppImages, Android .apk ) \u2014 and a Python / PySide6 backend in backend/ + main.py used for desktop development, prototyping and hardware hacking. Either backend owns all business logic, hardware I/O, network calls and data processing; the QML frontend in frontend/ owns rendering, animation and interaction, and works identically against either. \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 OCTAVE Application \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 Signals / Slots \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Backend (C++ src/ \u00b7or\u00b7 \u2502 \u25c4\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25ba \u2502 QML Frontend \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Python backend/) \u2502 Context Props \u2502 frontend/ \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 SettingsManager Clock \u2502 \u2502 Main.qml \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 DiagnosticsManager \u2502 \u2502 MainMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 VolumeController \u2502 \u2502 MediaRoom \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 MediaManager AudioAnalyzer\u2502 \u2502 MediaPlayer \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 SpotifyManager \u2502 \u2502 OBDMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 OBDManager (+ELM327) \u2502 \u2502 dashboards/ \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 NetworkManager \u2502 \u2502 gauges/ \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 DownloadManager \u2502 \u2502 CarMenu + \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 ESP32VolumeManager \u2502 \u2502 JeepVehicle \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 BerryIMUManager \u2502 \u2502 SensorMenu \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 GestureManager \u2502 \u2502 Settings/* \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 AndroidAutoManager \u2502 \u2502 BottomBar \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 PhoneMirrorManager \u2502 \u2502 ... \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 DashboardManager \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 UiWatchdog (no QML API) \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Video paths into the scene graph\u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 image://dhuframe (Android Auto)\u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 QVideoSink (Phone Mirror)\u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2502 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 Qt 6 runtime \u2014 QGuiApplication (C++) / QApplication (PySide6) \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2192 QQmlApplicationEngine \u2192 event loop \u2502 \u2502 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 \u2502 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 Backend / Frontend Split Layer Technology Location Responsibility Backend (shipped) C++17 / Qt 6.7.3 (CMake 3.21+) src/managers/ , src/util/ , src/phone_mirror/ , src/items/ , src/platform/ Business logic, hardware I/O, API calls, data processing, state \u2014 the only binary distributed on every platform, and the only backend on Android Backend (developer peer) Python 3.10+ / PySide6 6.8+ backend/ , main.py API-equivalent desktop backend (Linux / Windows / macOS / Raspberry Pi) for prototyping and hardware hacking with a REPL. Never packaged. Frontend QML (Qt Quick, Qt Quick 3D for the vehicle view) frontend/ UI rendering, animation, interaction, layout \u2014 loaded from disk at runtime by both backends (Android bundles it as assets:/frontend ) Bridge src/main.cpp \u00b7 main.py repo root / src/ Instantiates managers, registers context properties and custom types, wires cross-manager signals, owns shutdown The backend never touches the UI directly. The frontend never makes API calls or opens hardware. The only contract between them is the set of context-property names, signals, slots and properties \u2014 which is exactly why the two backends must expose the same surface (see Design Decisions ). Parity is the rule on desktop; mobile is C++-only Every C++ manager in src/managers/foo.{h,cpp} has a Python peer at backend/foo.py with the same class name, context-property name, slots, signals, properties and on-disk JSON formats. Android/iOS-specific code lives behind #ifdef Q_OS_ANDROID / Q_OS_MOBILE in C++ and has no Python mirror. QML must not know which backend is running beyond typeof fooManager !== \"undefined\" guards for features legitimately absent on one platform. The Manager Pattern Every backend feature is a manager class inheriting QObject . In C++ it exposes state through Q_PROPERTY , Q_INVOKABLE / public slots and signals; in Python through the Property , Slot and Signal decorators. The two spellings of the same thing: // C++ \u2014 src/managers/examplemanager.h class ExampleManager : public QObject { Q_OBJECT Q_PROPERTY( int value READ value NOTIFY valueChanged) public : int value () const { return m_value; } public slots : void setValue ( int v) { m_value = v; emit valueChanged(v); } signals : void valueChanged ( int ); private : int m_value = 0 ; }; # Python \u2014 backend/example_manager.py from PySide6.QtCore import QObject, Signal, Slot, Property class ExampleManager (QObject): valueChanged = Signal( int ) # QML listens to these def __init__ (self): super ().__init__() self._value = 0 @Slot( int ) # callable from QML def setValue (self, v): self._value"}, {"url": "audio-analyzer.html", "title": "Audio Analyzer", "subtitle": "FFT waveform visualization pipeline: audio decoding, frequency analysis, the 96-bar layout, and the data flow from file to QML in both backends", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Pipeline Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "backends", "text": "Backends", "level": 2}, {"id": "bar-count", "text": "Bar Count and the Single Toggle", "level": 2}, {"id": "decoding", "text": "Audio Decoding", "level": 2}, {"id": "fft-pipeline", "text": "FFT Pipeline", "level": 2}, {"id": "frequency-bands", "text": "Frequency Band Distribution", "level": 2}, {"id": "data-flow", "text": "Data Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals-slots", "text": "Signals and Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-integration", "text": "QML Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "performance", "text": "Performance Considerations", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Dependencies and Graceful Degradation", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Audio Analyzer - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Audio Analyzer Audio Analyzer FFT waveform visualization pipeline: audio decoding, frequency analysis, the 96-bar layout, and the data flow from file to QML in both backends Pipeline Overview backend/audio_analyzer.py (Python) and src/managers/audioanalyzer.{h,cpp} (C++) pre-compute FFT visualization data for an entire audio track on a worker thread, then serve time-indexed levels to QML based on the playback position. Nothing is computed in real time during playback. Both backends expose the same audioAnalyzer API, use 96 bars and 100 ms chunks, and produce the same 0-8 integer levels; where their internals differ this page says so. Audio File (.mp3/.flac/.m4a/...) | v +--------------------------------------+ | Decoder | Python: PyAV (av.open) -> all frames, | PyAV (Python) / QAudioDecoder (C++) | average channels to mono | | C++: QAudioDecoder asked for 8 kHz | | mono Int16, converted to float +--------------------------------------+ | v +------------------+ | Preprocessing | Normalize to [-1, 1] by the peak | | Python: decimate to ~8 kHz (every Nth sample) +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | FFT Analysis | Split into 100 ms chunks | numpy.fft.rfft | Hann window per chunk | / radix-2 C++ | Magnitudes, DC dropped | | 96 log-spaced bands (each >= 1 bin), mean per band +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | Normalization | 95th percentile of all band values = 1.0 | & Quantization | Power curve (^0.6) | | int(norm * 8) -> 0..8 +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | Stored as | chunks x 96 ints: _fft_data[chunk][bar] | _fft_data | 10 chunks per second of audio +------------------+ | v (during playback, 16 ms timer in MediaRoom) +------------------+ | update_position | chunk = int(seconds * 10) | | Emit fftDataChanged only if levels changed +------------------+ | v +------------------+ | QML Visualizer | 96 Rectangles, height = level / 8 | (MediaRoom.qml) | Behavior animations smooth the steps +------------------+ Backends Implementation Platforms Decoder FFT Worker src/managers/audioanalyzer.{h,cpp} All (C++) QAudioDecoder (Qt Multimedia: MediaCodec on Android, GStreamer/FFmpeg on Linux, Media Foundation on Windows, AVFoundation on macOS) Own in-place radix-2 Cooley-Tukey ( fft_radix2 ), no external dependency QtConcurrent::run() + QFutureWatcher backend/audio_analyzer.py Desktop PyAV ( av , FFmpeg bindings) numpy.fft.rfft ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=1) polled by a 100 ms QTimer Both are registered as the context property audioAnalyzer in src/main.cpp / main.py and consumed only by the waveform visualizer in frontend/MediaRoom.qml . Bar Count and the Single Toggle The visualizer renders a fixed 96 frequency bars ( m_numBars = 96 / _num_bars = 96 ) and MediaRoom polls the position every 16 ms (about 60 updates per second). Earlier versions had tiered quality settings; commit 118f484 collapsed them into the single showWaveformVisualizer setting (default true, see Settings Reference ). The MediaRoom container hard-codes what used to be the top tier: 96 bars, 16 ms timer, 120 ms InOutSine bar animations and pill-shaped \"fancy\" bars. There are no quality tiers left in either backend or the QML. Audio Decoding Python: PyAV The Python backend decodes with PyAV , so virtually any container FFmpeg understands works. Every frame is converted to an ndarray; multi-channel frames are averaged to mono: container = av. open (file_path) audio_stream = next ((s for s in container.streams if s.type == 'audio' ), None ) samples = [] sample_rate = audio_stream.rate or 44100 for frame in container. decode (audio_stream): frame_data = frame. to_ndarray () if len (frame_data.shape) > 1 : frame_data = frame_data. mean (axis= 0 ) # stereo -> mono samples. append (frame_data) container. close () Python preprocessing Concatenate - all decoded frames are joined into one float32 array Normalize - divide by the global maximum absolute value to get a [-1, 1] range Decimate - keep every Nth sample with N = sample_rate // 8000 , so a 44.1 kHz file becomes 8820 Hz and a 48 kHz file 8000 Hz ( effective_rate = sample_rate // N ). There is no anti-aliasing filter; for a visualizer that is acceptable and it cuts the FFT work roughly five-fold. all_samples = np. concatenate (samples). astype (np.float32) max_val = np. max (np. abs (all_samples)) if max_val > 0 : all_samples = all_samples / max_val downsample_factor = max ( 1 , sample_rate // 8000 ) all_samples = all_samples[::downsample_factor] effective_rate = sample_rate // downsample_factor C++: QAudioDecoder The C++ backend asks Qt Multimedia's QAudioDecoder for 8 kHz, mono, Int16 output directly, so the channel mix-down and resampling happen inside the platform decoder and the analyzer never sees the original rate. Int16 is requested rather than Float because some Android MediaCodec configurations reject float sample formats; the buffer is converted to normalized float32 ( \u00d7 1/32768 ) afterwards. Because the worker has"}, {"url": "bottom-bar.html", "title": "Bottom Bar", "subtitle": "The persistent navigation and media control bar: layout, dual orientation, volume routing through VolumeController, navigation button behaviour, the clock, and the update-available indicator", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "layout", "text": "Layout and Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "orientation", "text": "Dual Orientation", "level": 2}, {"id": "media-controls", "text": "Media Transport Controls", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume", "text": "Volume Slider and Mute", "level": 2}, {"id": "mute-states", "text": "Mute Icon States", "level": 2}, {"id": "navigation", "text": "Navigation Buttons", "level": 2}, {"id": "update-notification", "text": "Update Notification", "level": 2}, {"id": "clock", "text": "Clock Section", "level": 2}, {"id": "shuffle", "text": "Shuffle State", "level": 2}, {"id": "external-volume", "text": "External Volume Sources", "level": 2}, {"id": "managers", "text": "Manager Communication", "level": 2}, {"id": "visibility-popup", "text": "Toggle View Popup", "level": 2}, {"id": "sizing", "text": "Sizing Tokens", "level": 2}, {"id": "animation-pattern", "text": "Button Animation Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "svg-pattern", "text": "SVG Icon Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Bottom Bar - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Bottom Bar Bottom Bar The persistent navigation and media control bar: layout, dual orientation, volume routing through VolumeController, navigation button behaviour, the clock, and the update-available indicator Overview frontend/BottomBar.qml (about 3100 lines) is the persistent control surface that is visible on every page. It is a Rectangle with a themed gradient background whose contents are provided by a Loader that swaps between two complete layout components: a horizontal bar docked at the bottom of the window and a vertical bar docked at the left. Both layouts carry the same three logical sections \u2014 media transport controls, navigation buttons, and the clock. It is instantiated once in frontend/Main.qml with z: 0 ; the page StackView sits above it ( z: 1 ) and anchors to bottomBar.top (horizontal) or bottomBar.right (vertical). A 12 px gradient Rectangle ( bottomBarShadow / sideBarShadow ) in Main.qml draws a soft shadow along the bar's inner edge. Horizontal layout (bottomBarOrientation = \"bottom\"): +-----------------------------+-----------------------------------+--------------+ | SECTION 1 (40 % width) | SECTION 2 (fills remaining) | SECTION 3 | | Media controls | Navigation | (20 % width) | | [<<] [>||] [>>] [Shuf] [Mute] 50% [Home] [OBD] [Media] [Sensor] [Gear] [AA] [PM] | 12:34 | +-----------------------------+-----------------------------------+--------------+ Vertical layout (bottomBarOrientation = \"side\", 10 % of window width): +----------+ | [<<][>||][>>] SECTION 1 (top 40 % height) | [Shuf] | | [Mute] | | 50% | |----------| | [Home] | SECTION 2 (fills remaining) | [OBD] | | [Media] | | [Sensor] | | [Gear] | | [AA] | | [PM] | |----------| | 12:34 | SECTION 3 (bottom 10 % height) +----------+ Layout and Structure The root is a Rectangle whose gradient direction follows the orientation. Note the unusual stop positions \u2014 the \"start\" colour is placed at 1.5 , beyond the end of the gradient, so the visible bar is dominated by bottomBarGradientEnd (the theme's baseAlt ) fading toward base : Rectangle { id : bottomBar gradient : Gradient { orientation : isVertical ? Gradient.Horizontal : Gradient.Vertical GradientStop { position : 1.5 ; color : App.Style.bottomBarGradientStart } GradientStop { position : 0.0 ; color : App.Style.bottomBarGradientEnd } } required property StackView stackView required property Window mainWindow Loader { anchors.fill : parent sourceComponent : isVertical ? verticalLayoutComponent : horizontalLayoutComponent } } Like every other QML file in the project, the bar defines local dp() / dpMin() wrappers ( Math.round(size * (App.Spacing.effectiveScale || 1.0)) ) instead of calling App.Spacing.dp() . The comment at the top of the file explains why: calling functions on a QML singleton is unreliable on Android, so every file re-implements the two-line helper locally. Section 1: Media Controls In the horizontal layout this Item takes parent.width * 0.4 when settingsManager.showBottomBarMediaControls is true. When the user hides the media controls the item stays in the layout but its width is bound to clockSection.width , so the navigation row still sits at the true horizontal centre. The vertical layout uses the same trick with heights (40 % shown, 10 % hidden). Section 2: Navigation Uses Layout.fillWidth: true (or fillHeight when vertical) and centres a RowLayout / ColumnLayout of nav buttons. The horizontal row has z: 1 so that, with many buttons enabled, its outer buttons (Phone Mirror in particular) still receive clicks when the row is wider than its section. Its spacing is computed by fitSpacing : readonly property real preferredSpacing: App.Spacing.bottomBarBetweenButtonMargin * 6 readonly property real fitSpacing: { var n = 0 , w = 0 for ( var i = 0 ; i < children.length; ++i) { var c = children[i] if (c.visible && c.implicitWidth > 0 ) { n++; w += c.implicitWidth } } if (n < 2 ) return preferredSpacing var avail = parent.width - w return Math. max ( dp ( 4 ), Math. min (preferredSpacing, avail / (n - 1 ))) } The gap between buttons shrinks (down to dp(4) ) instead of letting the row overflow under the clock or the media controls. Section 3: Clock Horizontal: parent.width * 0.2 when settingsManager.showClock is true, otherwise 0 so the navigation row re-centres. Vertical: a fixed 10 % of the bar height at the bottom. Dual Orientation The isVertical property is bound to the persisted setting (Display \u2192 Layout \u2192 \"Nav Bar Position\", a SettingsSegmentedControl with the options \"bottom\" and \"side\" ): property bool isVertical: settingsManager && settingsManager.bottomBarOrientation === \"side\" updateLayout() re-anchors the bar. Anchors are released (set to undefined ) before the new ones are assigned, which avoids a transient state where conflicting anchors are active at the same time: function updateLayout () { if (isVertical) { anchors.bottom = undefined anchors.right = undefined anchors.left = parent.left anchors.top = parent.top wid"}, {"url": "building.html", "title": "Building &amp; Distribution", "subtitle": "CMake / Qt 6 build of the shipped C++ binary, per-platform packaging, the GitHub Actions matrix, the Python developer backend, and why the pipeline is shaped the way it is.", "headings": [{"id": "quick-start", "text": "Quick Start", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "cmake-options", "text": "CMake Options &amp; Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "windows", "text": "Windows Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "macos", "text": "macOS Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "linux", "text": "Linux Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "ci-cd", "text": "GitHub Actions CI/CD", "level": 2}, {"id": "android", "text": "Android APK Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-server", "text": "Phone Server Jar &amp; adb", "level": 2}, {"id": "python", "text": "Python Developer Backend", "level": 2}, {"id": "troubleshooting", "text": "Build Troubleshooting", "level": 2}], "body": "Building & Distribution - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Building & Distribution Building & Distribution CMake / Qt 6 build of the shipped C++ binary, per-platform packaging, the GitHub Actions matrix, the Python developer backend, and why the pipeline is shaped the way it is. Quick Start Every shipped binary (Windows .exe , macOS .dmg , Linux x86_64 and aarch64 AppImages, Android .apk ) is built from the C++ / Qt 6 tree in src/ with CMake. There is no PyInstaller pipeline, no .deb , no Flatpak and no Buildozer \u2014 the Python backend is never packaged (see below ). # Configure + build (Debug, current platform) cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug cmake --build build -j # Run ./build/octave # Linux/macOS (QML loads from the checkout's frontend/) ./build/octave --debug # the only C++ command-line flag: DEBUG-level logging + octave-cpp-debug.log ./build/Debug/octave.exe # Windows MSVC multi-config # App store build (downloads feature stripped out) cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DOCTAVE_ENABLE_DOWNLOADS=OFF cmake --build build -j Requirements (all desktops) CMake 3.21+ ( cmake_minimum_required in CMakeLists.txt ), Qt 6.7.3 (the CI-pinned version; other 6.7.x / 6.8.x will likely work but are untested), a C++17 compiler (GCC 11+ / Clang 14+ / MSVC 2022), TagLib (required on every desktop platform \u2014 find_package(taglib CONFIG) from vcpkg on Windows, pkg-config taglib elsewhere; Android skips it and uses Qt's metadata fallback), and Ninja recommended on Linux/macOS ( cmake -G Ninja ). FFmpeg (libavcodec/libavutil) and Qt Quick 3D are optional \u2014 see CMake Options . Full per-platform package lists live in BUILD.md . Design Decisions The build and release pipeline has been rewritten several times. The reasons behind its current shape are recorded here so they are not re-litigated by accident. Sources: CLAUDE.md , comments in CMakeLists.txt and .github/workflows/build.yml , and the commit messages named below. Why C++ is the only shipped binary Commit 8211ba8 (\"ci: drop Python build jobs \u2014 C++ is the only shipped binary\") removed the PyInstaller build-windows / build-macos / build-linux jobs. Every release artifact on every platform now comes from src/ , which gives one packaging story (CMake + Qt's own deploy tools), native performance on the Orange Pi / Raspberry Pi head unit, and a mobile path \u2014 the Android APK is a Qt-for-Android build; there is no Python-on-Android and the buildozer / python-for-android files must not be reintroduced ( CLAUDE.md ). The release job in the workflow carries the same note: \"The Python tree is a developer/tinkerer backend ... only C++ ships.\" Why the Python backend stays CLAUDE.md is explicit that the two backends are deliberate, not a half-finished migration: Python stays for \"tinkering, accessibility, rapid prototyping, and hardware hacking on Raspberry Pi / single-board setups where a Python REPL is invaluable\". The README makes the same argument (\"On a Pi with a new sensor on the I\u00b2C bus, a Python REPL beats a compile cycle\"). The cost is the parity rule \u2014 every desktop feature lands in both trees in the same change set \u2014 and CI keeps the Python side honest with lint and test on every push even though nothing Python is packaged. The original Python\u2192C++ migration plan ( .claude/plans/wondrous-toasting-biscuit.md ) is superseded. Why AppImage rather than .deb on Linux v0.9 briefly shipped a .deb (commit fdb7d94 ). It \"installed cleanly but launched a window-less process\": the package staged the QML tree at usr/share/octave/frontend/ , which the binary's applicationDirPath()/../frontend lookup in src/main.cpp never finds. Commit 14811c5 replaced the .deb job with packaging/linux/build-appimage.sh , whose AppDir layout mirrors the runtime lookup exactly ( usr/bin/octave + usr/frontend/ ), and b9ca3be switched the docs. An AppImage also carries the pinned Qt 6.7.3 with it \u2014 distro Qt 6.2 on Ubuntu 22.04 cannot run the frontend at all (see CMake Options ) \u2014 and the same script produces the aarch64 image for the Orange Pi with no packaging-format work per distro. Flatpak and app-store variants remain deferred ( TODO/ci-multiplatform-builds.md ). Why the Linux artifact matters more than the others The vehicle head unit is an Orange Pi 5 Plus (aarch64). The cpp-build-linux-arm64 job exists specifically so that board gets a first-class binary built against the same Qt 6.7.3 as every other target (workflow comment: \"the actual vehicle hardware\"). It needed two extra decisions: Google publishes no aarch64 adb , so that build relies on the distro's adb on PATH, and the arm64 Qt package's GStreamer media plugin depends on libgstphotography , which jammy does not ship \u2014 OCTAVE uses Qt's FFmpeg media backend anyway, so the plugin is deleted after the CMake configure and before linuxdeploy-plugin-qt walks plugins/ ( OCTAVE_DROP_QT_PLUGINS , commits 39dd25b and 10a6afa ; it must be after configure because Qt's CMake config refuses to configure when a listed plugin file is missing)."}, {"url": "components.html", "title": "Components", "subtitle": "Reusable top-level QML components: AlbumArtCarousel, CoverflowCard, ThemedIcon, TerminalFeedback, CornerBrackets, GalleryTile, SensorCard, GForceDisplay, HomeOBDView, the OBDParameterModel singleton and the rigged Jeep TJ", "headings": [{"id": "scope", "text": "Scope and Conventions", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-carousel", "text": "AlbumArtCarousel", "level": 2}, {"id": "coverflow-card", "text": "CoverflowCard", "level": 2}, {"id": "themed-icon", "text": "ThemedIcon", "level": 2}, {"id": "terminal-feedback", "text": "TerminalFeedback", "level": 2}, {"id": "corner-brackets", "text": "CornerBrackets", "level": 2}, {"id": "gallery-tile", "text": "GalleryTile", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensor-card", "text": "SensorCard", "level": 2}, {"id": "gforce-display", "text": "GForceDisplay", "level": 2}, {"id": "home-obd-view", "text": "HomeOBDView", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd-parameter-model", "text": "OBDParameterModel", "level": 2}, {"id": "jeep-rig", "text": "The Jeep TJ Rig", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Components - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Components Components Reusable top-level QML components: AlbumArtCarousel, CoverflowCard, ThemedIcon, TerminalFeedback, CornerBrackets, GalleryTile, SensorCard, GForceDisplay, HomeOBDView, the OBDParameterModel singleton and the rigged Jeep TJ Scope and Conventions This page covers the reusable QML types that live at the top of frontend/ . The settings component library ( settings/ : cards, tiles, toggles, sliders, chips, hub widgets) is documented in Settings UI ; the gauge primitives ( gauges/ ) and the dashboard widgets ( dashboards/widgets/ ) in Gauges & Dashboards ; the design-token singletons ( Style , Spacing , EnvironmentTheme ) in Theme System . Full views are on the Pages page. Because frontend/ is on the QML import path, every file here is usable by its type name without an import. Files that import \".\" as App reach the singletons as App.Style / App.Spacing / App.EnvironmentTheme . Almost every component carries the same two-line local dp() / dpMin() wrapper (see GUI Scale for why). Component Root type Instantiated by AlbumArtCarousel Item MediaRoom.qml (and, through it, the Now Playing Studio preview) CoverflowCard Item AlbumArtCarousel (three instances) ThemedIcon Item settings tiles and cards, hub widgets TerminalFeedback Rectangle settings/MediaSettingsPage.qml (library-scan and Spotify-connection output); OBDDiagnostics.qml has its own inline terminal instead CornerBrackets Item TerminalFeedback , SettingsCard , SettingsTile , SettingsDashboardCard , SettingsHubCard , SettingsHubGridCard (always hidden, see below) GalleryTile Rectangle the Primitives Gallery popup in OBDMenu.qml SensorCard , GForceDisplay Rectangle SensorMenu.qml HomeOBDView Item MainMenu.qml OBDParameterModel QtObject singleton every OBD view, the gauges, the dashboard editor, Main.qml's IMU gating JeepVehicle , JeepHinge , JeepLamp , JeepLink Quick3D Node CarMenu.qml AlbumArtCarousel frontend/AlbumArtCarousel.qml (938 lines) is the album-art display at the centre of MediaRoom . It owns three CoverflowCard s (previous, centre, next), a frozen \"exit snapshot\" of the outgoing art, the dissolve shader, and ten selectable track-change animations. The header comment states the design: one unified transition list \u2014 transitionStyle picks any of the ten names, and each animation reads previewEnabled to decide whether the side cards take part (3D-flavoured animations animate them when the preview is on and no-op that part when it is off; 2D-flavoured animations never touch them). Public API // Inputs property int direction: 1 // +1 next, -1 previous property url artSource: \"\" property url prevArtSource: \"\" property url nextArtSource: \"\" property string transitionStyle: \"Crossfade\" property bool previewEnabled: false // show 3D side cards property bool roundedArt: false property real artRadius: 0 property bool vinylMode: false property real sideCardAngle: 30 property real sideCardOpacity: 0.4 property bool showShadow: false property real playbackPosition: 0 // vinyl rotation property real playbackDuration: 0 // Outputs property bool animBusy: false readonly property bool dissolveActive readonly property real dissolveProgress readonly property url dissolveNewArt readonly property real artSize // min(w,h) * (previewEnabled ? 0.75 : 0.85) signal animationFinished () signal settled () // 50 ms after animationFinished, animBusy is false again signal artClicked () signal artLoadFailed () function triggerSlide () // dispatch on transitionStyle function showSideCards () // re-bind side-card opacity after a 3D animation MediaRoom binds the inputs straight to settings: show3DAlbumPreview , roundedAlbumArt , albumArtCornerRadius (through dp() ), vinylRecordMode , sideCardAngle , sideCardOpacity , showAlbumArtShadow and albumArtTransition . There is no separate \"3D style\" setting; the same albumArtTransition string selects a 2D or 3D animation. Transition styles and constants Style Flavour Constants (top of file) Notes \"Crossfade\" (default and fallback) 2D kCrossfadeDuration 250 , kCrossfadeScale 0.97 Old art fades out while the new art scales up from 0.97 \"Slide\" 2D kSlideDuration 280 , kSlideDistance 80 (passed through dp() ) Direction-aware horizontal slide \"Vinyl Lift\" 2D kLiftDuration 180 , kLiftPause 60 , kLiftScale 0.85 Sequential: lift out, pause, drop in with OutBack overshoot 1.2 \u2014 420 ms in total \"Dissolve\" 2D kDissolveDuration 900 , kDissolveGridSize 20 , kDissolveSmoothness 0.1 GPU shader, see below \"Flip\" 2D kFlipDuration 350 (half per side) Y-axis flip: exit snapshot turns away, new art turns in \"Coverflow\" , \"Conveyor\" , \"Stack\" , \"Depth\" , \"Swing\" 3D k3dDuration 350 , k3dSideOffsetFactor 0.42 , k3dSideScale 0.72 , k3dFarScale 0.5 Side cards move into the centre slot; a few per-style numbers are inline (Coverflow fast-fade 220 ms, Swing \u00b170\u00b0 exit / \u00b160\u00b0 side, Depth 0.5 exit / 0.4 far scale) Every animation sets animBusy = true on start and calls animationFinished() on finish; a 50 ms settleT"}, {"url": "development.html", "title": "Development Guide", "subtitle": "Dev mode, the mock OBD simulator, the command server and MCP servers, profiling, screenshots and video stories, the parity workflow for adding features, testing, CI, the deferred-work folder \u2014 and why the tooling is built this way.", "headings": [{"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "repo-map", "text": "Repository Map for Developers", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-mode", "text": "Dev Mode Setup &amp; Usage", "level": 2}, {"id": "mock-obd", "text": "Mock OBD Manager", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-tools", "text": "DevToolsPanel", "level": 2}, {"id": "command-server", "text": "Command Server (TCP)", "level": 2}, {"id": "mcp-server", "text": "MCP Servers", "level": 2}, {"id": "profiling", "text": "Performance Profiling", "level": 2}, {"id": "screenshots", "text": "Screenshots &amp; Video Stories", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-features", "text": "How to Add New Features (Parity Workflow)", "level": 2}, {"id": "testing", "text": "Testing &amp; Linting", "level": 2}, {"id": "ci", "text": "Continuous Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "todo", "text": "Deferred Work: the TODO Folder", "level": 2}], "body": "Development Guide - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Development Guide Development Guide Dev mode, the mock OBD simulator, the command server and MCP servers, profiling, screenshots and video stories, the parity workflow for adding features, testing, CI, the deferred-work folder \u2014 and why the tooling is built this way. Design Decisions Two backends, one frontend The C++ tree ( src/ ) is what ships; the Python tree ( backend/ , main.py ) is the development and hardware-hacking backend. Both expose the same managers, context-property names, slots, signals, properties and on-disk JSON to the shared QML in frontend/ , which must never know which one is running (the only allowed difference is a typeof fooManager !== \"undefined\" guard for a feature legitimately absent on one platform). This is the standing policy in CLAUDE.md ; the rationale and the packaging consequences are on the Building page. Practically, it means the Python side is where most tooling lives: the command server, perf monitor, mock OBD and MCP servers are Python, and they exercise the same QML the C++ binary runs. Why dev mode wraps production instead of duplicating it python -m dev.main_dev imports main , which runs the whole production start-up (app, engine, every manager, every signal connection) and stops short of loading the QML because that lives under main.py 's if __name__ == \"__main__\" guard. Dev mode then swaps in the mock OBD, registers its context properties, calls main.load_main_qml() and reparents an overlay into the real window. The docstring in dev/main_dev.py records why: the old dev/dev_main.py \"duplicated 85% of main.py and constantly drifted out of parity\". Same code path as production, extra layers bolted on afterwards. Why a TCP command server and MCP servers for performance work The head unit runs in a Jeep; the problems that matter are stutter, stalls and regressions that only show up when the app is driven the way a driver drives it. A JSON-over-TCP command server ( backend/command_server.py , only started by --profile ) lets a script or an agent navigate pages, toggle every setting, skip tracks, drive the simulator and read the perf counters while the perf monitor measures the event loop, so a test is repeatable and comparable between commits ( dev/docs/perf_test_plan.md , dev/perf/run_perf_loop.py ). The two MCP servers expose the same channel to a coding agent so it can reproduce a report, bisect a regression or benchmark a setting without a human clicking. The README makes the same point: \"The same command channel is exposed as MCP tools, so a coding agent can navigate the app, change settings, read performance counters and record clips.\" Why the tooling is gitignored dev/ , .mcp.json , backend/command_server.py , backend/perf_monitor.py and backend/perf_patches.py are all in .gitignore (\"Developer mode tools (completely isolated from main codebase)\", \"Performance profiling\"). They are synced between the developer machines through Dropbox, not git, so the shipped tree carries no simulator, no TCP listener and no monkey-patching; main.py imports them lazily and only under --profile , and every one of those imports is guarded: on a fresh clone --profile logs a warning per missing module and starts without the monitor / command server rather than raising ImportError . Dev mode itself ( dev/ ) is simply absent there \u2014 see Troubleshooting . Why screenshots are grabbed from inside Qt The first screenshot tool used xdotool + mss (X11 input and capture) and broke under Wayland. The current one asks the running app to capture itself: screenshot and record_start run root.grabWindow() on the GUI thread, which works identically on Wayland, X11 and Xvfb, and hides the dev HUD automatically. It also gives deterministic window sizes for README assets ( dev/docs/SCREENSHOTS.md ). Why ruff runs warn-only The full ruleset ( E F W E722 B UP ) is enabled in pyproject.toml , but the CI step is ruff check . || true because the legacy tree still has noise (long lines, import ordering, unused re-exports \u2014 already ignored as E501 E402 F401 B008 UP007 , with more remaining). Only the two rules that indicate a real bug, E722 (bare except) and F821 (undefined name), are blocking. The workflow step is labelled \"warn-only \u2014 full ruleset has legacy noise\"; CLAUDE.md calls this the Phase 1 cleanup and expects E722 / F821 to stay blocking while the enforced set widens as the code is cleaned up. Why errors do not surface in the UI Every backend routes errors to logs and status text, never to pop-ups, because a notification flickering in a driver's peripheral vision is a distraction risk ( TODO/error-notification-ui.md ). The trade-off is that developers must be able to get at the logs after the fact, which is why both backends have rotating files, crash handlers, a UI watchdog and the Diagnostics card ( Troubleshooting ). Repository Map for Developers Path Tracked? What it is src/ , CMakeLists.txt yes C++ backend: main.cpp , managers/ , util/ (logger, UI "}, {"url": "download-manager.html", "title": "Download Manager", "subtitle": "YouTube Music search and yt-dlp downloads on both backends, including the Android JNI path and the app-store stub", "headings": [], "body": "Download Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Download Manager Download Manager YouTube Music search and yt-dlp downloads on both backends, including the Android JNI path and the app-store stub Overview The DownloadManager lets the user search YouTube Music from the head unit and save songs into the local media library, where MediaManager picks them up. It is registered as the QML context property downloadManager and driven by frontend/DownloadPage.qml . The two backends reach the same result with different machinery: Python ( backend/download_manager.py ) wraps the vendored music_dl engine ( backend/music_dl/ , a trimmed fork of spotdl) that drives yt-dlp and ffmpeg as Python libraries. C++ ( src/managers/downloadmanager.{h,cpp} ) spawns the yt-dlp executable with QProcess , runs a small Python helper ( scripts/ytmusic_search.py ) for search, and tags files with TagLib. On Android the same class talks to the youtubedl-android runtime over JNI. No Spotify Credentials Required Search goes to YouTube Music ( ytmusicapi ) on both backends, so Spotify credentials are optional. When they are present, the Python engine additionally resolves pasted Spotify track/album/playlist URLs to songs with full Spotify metadata (ISRC-aware matching). The C++ backend has no Spotify path at all. System Architecture Python: DownloadManager (backend/download_manager.py) | |-- MusicDL engine (lazy init on first search/download) | |-- SpotifyClient (optional, spotipy client-credentials) | |-- Downloader | | |-- providers/audio/ytmusic.py YouTubeMusic (ytmusicapi search) | | |-- providers/audio/base.py yt-dlp YoutubeDL (library call) | | |-- utils/ffmpeg.py conversion | | |-- utils/metadata.py mutagen tag + cover embedding | | |-- download/progress_handler.py callback -> _on_download_progress() | |-- utils/search.py get_ytm_client(), Spotify URL parsing | |-- ThreadPoolExecutor (2 workers) search + downloads |-- QTimer _poll_timer (200 ms) drains progress queue, resolves futures |-- _progress_updates + _progress_lock C++ desktop: DownloadManager (src/managers/downloadmanager.cpp) | |-- search: QProcess python3 scripts/ytmusic_search.py <query> 25 | (fallback: yt-dlp --dump-json ytsearch25:<query>) |-- download: QProcess yt-dlp ... -o \"<dir>/%(artist,uploader)s - %(title)s.%(ext)s\" | stdout parsed live for \"[download] NN.N%\" (--newline) |-- post: TagLib title/artist/album + QNetworkAccessManager cover -> APIC/covr/PICTURE | |-- QMap<songId, DownloadTask> m_downloadTasks, QQueue m_downloadQueue |-- m_maxConcurrentDownloads = 2 C++ Android ( Q_OS_MOBILE ): DownloadManager |-- YtDlpProcess = AndroidProcessAdapter (QProcess-shaped, runs on a QThread) | '-- OctaveAndroid::runYtDlp(args) --JNI--> OctaveMediaBridge.runYtDlp | '-- youtubedl-android YoutubeDL.execute() |-- search: QtConcurrent::run(OctaveAndroid::runYtMusicSearch) -> ytmusic_search.py under the AAR's Python |-- m_mobileProgressPoller (250 ms) -> OctaveAndroid::pollProgress() |-- cover: OctaveAndroid::runEmbedCover -> scripts/embed_cover.py (mutagen) The music_dl Engine (Python) backend/music_dl/ is OCTAVE's fork of spotdl ( __version__ = \"4.4.3\" in _version.py ; MIT licence, copyright 2021 spotDL Developers, kept in backend/music_dl/LICENSE ). The fork strips the CLI, the web UI and the Rich terminal progress bars: download/progress_handler.py is a \"callback-based progress handler\" that invokes a function instead of drawing a TUI, and the only audio provider left is providers/audio/ytmusic.py ( DOWNLOADER_OPTIONS[\"audio_providers\"] = [\"youtube-music\"] in utils/config.py ). Commit 5fec05d removed Pillow, python-slugify, typing_extensions and text-unidecode from the dependency lists. Entry Point: MusicDL Class class MusicDL : def __init__ ( self , client_id, client_secret, user_auth= False , cache_path= None , no_cache= False , headless= False , downloader_settings= None ): # Initialize spotify client only if credentials are provided self .has_spotify = bool (client_id and client_secret) if self .has_spotify: SpotifyClient .init(client_id=client_id, client_secret=client_secret, ...) self .downloader = Downloader (settings=downloader_settings) def search ( self , query): # List[str] -> List[Song] (parse_query) def download ( self , song): # -> Tuple[Song, Optional[Path]] def download_songs ( self , songs): # -> List[Tuple[Song, Optional[Path]]] def cleanup ( self ): # SpotifyClient.reset() when used Settings passed by DownloadManager _ensure_music_dl() builds the engine on first use with these downloader_settings (every other key is filled from DOWNLOADER_OPTIONS in utils/config.py ): downloader_settings = { \"output\" : os.path.join(download_path, \"{artists} - {title}.{output-ext}\" ), \"format\" : dl_format, # downloadFormat setting, default \"mp3\" \"bitrate\" : bitrate, # downloadBitrate setting, default \"auto\" \"threads\" : 2 , \"overwrite\" : \"skip\" , \"_progress_callback\" : self ._on_download_progress, } if cookies_path: downloader_settings[ \"cookie_file\" ] = cookies_path The Downloade"}, {"url": "frontend-overview.html", "title": "Frontend Overview", "subtitle": "QML architecture, the file inventory under frontend/, Main.qml structure, page routing, scaling, effects, shaders, and the bridge between the two backends and the UI", "headings": [{"id": "architecture", "text": "QML Architecture Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "file-inventory", "text": "File Inventory", "level": 2}, {"id": "main-qml", "text": "Main.qml Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "window-management", "text": "Window Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "page-routing", "text": "Page Routing Mechanism", "level": 2}, {"id": "imu-gating", "text": "IMU Rate Gating from the StackView", "level": 2}, {"id": "import-path", "text": "Import Path and Singletons", "level": 2}, {"id": "controls-style", "text": "Qt Quick Controls \"Basic\" Style", "level": 2}, {"id": "context-properties", "text": "Accessing Backend Managers (Context Properties)", "level": 2}, {"id": "typeof-guards", "text": "Guarding Backend Differences with typeof", "level": 2}, {"id": "gui-scale", "text": "GUI Scale", "level": 2}, {"id": "font-loading", "text": "Dynamic Font Loading", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-capture", "text": "Album Art Capture Pipeline", "level": 2}, {"id": "shift-light", "text": "Shift Light Overlays", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-connections", "text": "Settings Manager Connections", "level": 2}, {"id": "drop-shadows", "text": "Drop Shadows", "level": 2}, {"id": "graphical-effects", "text": "Graphical Effects: Qt5Compat vs MultiEffect", "level": 2}, {"id": "shaders", "text": "Shaders", "level": 2}, {"id": "objectnames", "text": "objectName Conventions for Dev Scripting", "level": 2}, {"id": "keyboard", "text": "Keyboard Handling", "level": 2}, {"id": "initialization", "text": "Initialization Sequence", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Frontend Overview - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Frontend Overview Frontend Overview QML architecture, the file inventory under frontend/ , Main.qml structure, page routing, scaling, effects, shaders, and the bridge between the two backends and the UI QML Architecture Overview OCTAVE's frontend is Qt Quick / QML. Most files import QtQuick 2.15 ; the 3D vehicle files ( CarMenu.qml , JeepVehicle.qml , JeepHinge.qml , JeepLamp.qml , JeepLink.qml ) use unversioned Qt 6 imports and QtQuick3D . The QML layer handles all visual presentation, animation, and interaction. Business logic lives in backend manager classes \u2014 C++ / Qt 6 in src/ (the shipped binary) or the parallel Python / PySide6 backend in backend/ \u2014 exposed to QML as context properties . The same frontend/ directory is loaded at runtime by both backends; no QML file knows which one is running. ApplicationWindow (Main.qml) +--------------------------------------------------------------+ | mainContainer (Item, anchors.fill) | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | StackView id: stackView objectName: \"stackView\" z:1 | | | | initialItem: MainMenu | | | | pushed lazily: MediaRoom, MediaPlayer, DownloadPage, | | | | OBDHome, OBDMenu, OBDDiagnostics, SensorHome, | | | | SensorMenu, CarMenu, ClockMenu, SettingsMenu, | | | | PhoneMirrorView, AndroidAutoView, | | | | dashboards/editor/DashboardEditor | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | bottomBarShadow / sideBarShadow (gradient strips) z:2 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | BottomBar (persistent nav + transport + volume) z:0 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | NowPlayingStudio (window-level overlay) z:5 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | | | globalFullScreenFlash (Rectangle) z:999999 | | | | globalAuraFlash (4 edge gradients) z:999999 | | | +--------------------------------------------------------+ | +--------------------------------------------------------------+ | FolderListModel (assets/fonts) + dynamic FontLoaders | | Timers: navReloadTimer (150 ms), globalFlashTimer | +--------------------------------------------------------------+ Key architectural points: Single window \u2014 one ApplicationWindow hosts everything, including full-window overlays (Now Playing Studio, shift-light flash). There are no secondary windows or dialogs. StackView navigation \u2014 pages are pushed/popped on a StackView ; there is no router. Every page except MainMenu is instantiated on demand with Qt.createComponent() . Persistent BottomBar \u2014 always visible below (horizontal) or to the left of (vertical \"side\" mode) the StackView. See Bottom Bar . Singletons declared in qmldir \u2014 Style , Spacing , EnvironmentTheme , OBDParameterModel , and dashboards/WidgetCatalog are pragma Singleton objects. No page transitions \u2014 all six StackView transitions ( pushEnter/Exit , popEnter/Exit , replaceEnter/Exit ) are null . No global z-order tricks beyond the ones in the diagram: the StackView sits above the BottomBar ( z: 1 vs 0 ), shadows at z: 2 , the studio at z: 5 , overlays at z: 999999 . File Inventory Everything the UI needs lives under frontend/ . The top level is flat; only settings, gauges, dashboards, shaders and assets get subdirectories. File Role Documented in Main.qml ApplicationWindow, StackView, overlays, font scan, shift light, navigation helpers this page qmldir Registers the five singletons (see Import Path ) this page Style.qml , Spacing.qml , EnvironmentTheme.qml Design-token singletons: colors/fonts/themes, scaled dimensions, structural tokens Theme System , GUI Scale BottomBar.qml Persistent navigation, transport, volume Bottom Bar MainMenu.qml , HomeOBDView.qml Home screen and its OBD card grid Pages , Components MediaRoom.qml , MediaPlayer.qml , DownloadPage.qml Now-playing view, library browser, YouTube Music search/download Pages NowPlayingStudio.qml Window-level immersive editor for Now Playing visuals Pages AlbumArtCarousel.qml , CoverflowCard.qml , ThemedIcon.qml , GalleryTile.qml , CornerBrackets.qml , TerminalFeedback.qml Reusable components Components OBDHome.qml , OBDMenu.qml , OBDDiagnostics.qml , OBDParameterModel.qml OBD hub, live gauges/dashboards, DTC reader, PID singleton Pages , Components SensorHome.qml , SensorMenu.qml , SensorCard.qml , GForceDisplay.qml Sensor hub, IMU readouts, sensor cards Pages , Components CarMenu.qml , JeepVehicle.qml , JeepHinge.qml , JeepLamp.qml , JeepLink.qml , JeepSteering.js Quick3D vehicle-attitude view and the rigged Jeep TJ Pages , Components ClockMenu.qml Digital + analog clock page (reached only through navigateTo ; no in-app button) Pages PhoneMirrorView.qml , AndroidAutoView.qml Phone mirroring and Android Auto DHU views Pages , Phone Mirror & Android Auto SettingsMenu.qml + SettingsHubLayout.qml , SettingsSidebarLayout.qml , SettingsCarouselLayout.qml , SettingsDashboardLayout.qml Settings orchestrator and its four "}, {"url": "gauges-dashboards.html", "title": "Gauges &amp; Dashboards", "subtitle": "Reusable gauge primitives, the OBDParameterModel binding layer, schema-1 JSON dashboards rendered by DashboardRenderer, DashboardManager (C++ and Python), and the in-app drag-and-tap dashboard editor", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "files", "text": "Files at a Glance", "level": 2}, {"id": "parameter-model", "text": "Data Flow: OBDParameterModel", "level": 2}, {"id": "binding-api", "text": "Shared Binding API", "level": 2}, {"id": "primitives", "text": "Gauge Primitives", "level": 2}, {"id": "self-binding", "text": "Self-Binding Widgets", "level": 2}, {"id": "angle-math", "text": "Angle and Needle Math", "level": 2}, {"id": "schema", "text": "Dashboard JSON Schema (schema 1)", "level": 2}, {"id": "presets", "text": "Built-in Presets", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboard-manager", "text": "DashboardManager (C++ and Python)", "level": 2}, {"id": "renderer", "text": "DashboardRenderer: Spec to QML", "level": 2}, {"id": "widget-catalog", "text": "WidgetCatalog", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdmenu", "text": "OBDMenu Integration: Active Dashboard and Chooser", "level": 2}, {"id": "editor", "text": "Dashboard Editor Internals", "level": 2}, {"id": "validation", "text": "Validation and Hot Reload", "level": 2}, {"id": "testing", "text": "Testing", "level": 2}, {"id": "authoring", "text": "Authoring New Gauges and Dashboards", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Gauges & Dashboards - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Gauges & Dashboards Gauges & Dashboards Reusable gauge primitives, the OBDParameterModel binding layer, schema-1 JSON dashboards rendered by DashboardRenderer, DashboardManager (C++ and Python), and the in-app drag-and-tap dashboard editor Overview OCTAVE's OBD view has two presentations: the built-in Parameter Cards grid inside frontend/OBDMenu.qml , and dashboards -- JSON specs that place gauge primitives on a grid. Five presets ship in frontend/dashboards/presets/ ; users build their own in an in-app editor, or drop a JSON file into the user dashboards directory. DashboardManager (C++ src/managers/dashboardmanager.{h,cpp} , Python backend/dashboard_manager.py , full parity) enumerates both sources, watches the user directory, and exposes save / delete / duplicate. frontend/dashboards/DashboardRenderer.qml turns a spec into live widgets; the chooser popup shows every dashboard as a scaled live miniature; the selection persists as the activeDashboard setting. Every gauge binds to the OBDParameterModel singleton by paramId ( \"RPM\" , \"SPEED\" , \"LATERAL_G\" ...) -- title, unit, min and max are auto-populated from the model and the live value updates reactively. No gauge touches obdManager directly, so the whole system is backend-agnostic and works unchanged with the Python or C++ OBD manager , the dev-mode mock, or the editor's demo data. The step-by-step authoring spec is docs/GAUGE_AUTHORING.md ; this page documents how the pieces are built and why. Where the two disagree, the code wins and both should be updated. Files at a Glance frontend/ OBDParameterModel.qml singleton: allParameters (92 ids + kinds), paramValues, simulation OBDMenu.qml Parameter Cards grid, DashboardRenderer host, chooser popup, gallery qmldir registers OBDParameterModel and WidgetCatalog as singletons gauges/ CircularGauge.qml arc + ticks + optional needle + centre readout ArcGauge.qml top half-dial with readout below the chord BarGauge.qml horizontal / vertical filled bar with warn threshold LinearGauge.qml scale with bidirectional fill from zero DigitalReadout.qml big number + title + unit SparklineGauge.qml timer-sampled rolling history line (Canvas) WarningLight.qml threshold tell-tale GForceGauge.qml self-binding LATERAL_G / LONGITUDINAL_G dot + trail CompassGauge.qml self-binding HEADING compass card dashboards/ DashboardRenderer.qml spec -> absolutely positioned Loaders WidgetCatalog.qml singleton: type -> url, palette meta, supportedKinds, editableProps presets/ sport, minimal, fullgrid, performance, tj-wrangler (.json) widgets/ NowPlayingWidget.qml self-binding media card MediaControlsWidget.qml self-binding transport buttons editor/ DashboardEditor.qml page: owns the working spec, save / cancel / draft / demo EditorCanvas.qml [+] targets, selection, drag-to-move, occupancy map PalettePopup.qml widget palette (reads WidgetCatalog) PIDPicker.qml searchable, kind-filtered PID list PropertiesPanel.qml PID / position / span / curated props inspector src/managers/dashboardmanager.{h,cpp} C++ DashboardManager backend/dashboard_manager.py Python DashboardManager docs/GAUGE_AUTHORING.md authoring spec (source of truth for the API) dev/tests/drive_dashboard_editor.py end-to-end editor scenario (command server) Data Flow: OBDParameterModel obdManager / berryIMU -- *Changed(float) --> OBDParameterModel -- paramValues[id] --> gauge bindings (either backend) (92 handlers) _pendingValues _normalized 33 ms flush timer _animNorm (Behavior) frontend/OBDParameterModel.qml (registered as a singleton in frontend/qmldir ) declares: Member Description allParameters Array of { id, title, unit, min, max, kind } : 92 entries -- 84 OBD ids (the 18 default-enabled including IGNITION_TIMING , plus 66 extended) and 8 BerryIMU ids. docs/GAUGE_AUTHORING.md and several QML comments say 93; the array has 92. kind One of percentage (0-100 %), temperature (\u00b0C), voltage , pressure (kPa/Pa), bidirectional (signed, straddles zero: trims, timing, EGR error, WR currents, pitch/roll/G), numeric (everything else). Widgets declare which kinds they accept; the PID picker filters on it. originalParameters , isOriginalParameter(id) The 18 default-enabled ids. shortTitles , parameterInfo , getParamInfo(id) Compact metadata ( SPD , CLT , AFR ...) keyed by id for the home cards; getParamInfo returns a permissive fallback for unknown ids. hasParameter(id) Strict membership test, added for renderer/editor validation precisely because getParamInfo never fails. paramValues , getParamValue(id) Live values. A plain object that is replaced (never mutated) so bindings re-evaluate; missing ids read as 0. updateParamValue(id, v) Writes into _pendingValues and arms the one-shot 33 ms _flushTimer ; the flush does Object.assign({}, paramValues, _pendingValues) once. This coalescing landed in 486a809 after the IMU's ~440 signals/s pegged the QML thread at 100 % on an idle Orange Pi. simulationActive Demo mode: a 100 ms timer sweeps every par"}, {"url": "getting-started.html", "title": "Getting Started", "subtitle": "Three ways in: install a pre-built release (C++), run the Python developer backend from a checkout,\n            or build the C++ tree yourself. First run and directory layout for all of them.", "headings": [{"id": "which-path", "text": "Which Path Do I Want?", "level": 2}, {"id": "requirements", "text": "System Requirements", "level": 2}, {"id": "install-release", "text": "Installing a Pre-built Release", "level": 2}, {"id": "quick-start", "text": "Python Quick Start", "level": 2}, {"id": "setup-script", "text": "What setup.py Does", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-linux", "text": "Linux (Python backend)", "level": 2}, {"id": "linux-arch", "text": "Arch Linux / Manjaro / EndeavourOS", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-debian", "text": "Debian / Ubuntu / Mint / Pop!_OS", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-fedora", "text": "Fedora / RHEL / CentOS / Rocky", "level": 3}, {"id": "linux-rpi", "text": "Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi", "level": 3}, {"id": "platform-windows", "text": "Windows (Python backend)", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-macos", "text": "macOS (Python backend)", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-wsl", "text": "WSL Notes", "level": 2}, {"id": "cpp-build", "text": "Building the C++ Backend", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform-android", "text": "Android Build", "level": 2}, {"id": "running", "text": "Running OCTAVE", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-mode", "text": "Developer Mode", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Python Dependencies", "level": 2}, {"id": "directory-structure", "text": "Directory Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "first-steps", "text": "First Steps After Install", "level": 2}], "body": "Getting Started - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Getting Started Getting Started Three ways in: install a pre-built release (C++), run the Python developer backend from a checkout, or build the C++ tree yourself. First run and directory layout for all of them. Which Path Do I Want? I want to\u2026 Use Section put OCTAVE in a car or just run it a pre-built release binary (C++ / Qt 6) Installing a Pre-built Release hack on it, prototype a feature, poke a sensor from a REPL the Python / PySide6 backend from a checkout Python Quick Start change the shipped code, or build for Android the C++ tree with CMake Building the C++ Backend / Android Build Whichever backend you run, the QML frontend in frontend/ is the same and the settings file is shared, so you can switch between them on one machine. System Requirements Requirement Minimum Notes OS Windows 10+, macOS, Linux (x86_64 or aarch64), Android 11+ Raspberry Pi OS / Orange Pi (Debian-based) supported. macOS releases are built on macOS 14 against Qt 6.7.3. Python (dev backend only) 3.10+ pyproject.toml declares requires-python = \">=3.10\" with no upper cap and setup.py exits below 3.10; CI tests 3.11 and the developer machines run 3.14. Not needed for release binaries or Android. C++ toolchain (source builds only) CMake 3.21+, Qt 6.7.3, a C++17 compiler GCC 11+ / Clang 14+ / MSVC 2022. Qt modules: Multimedia, NetworkAuth, SerialPort, Bluetooth/Connectivity, Qt5Compat, ShaderTools, optionally Quick3D. See Building . Display Any resolution Auto-scales from a 720-pixel baseline (Python: screen height / 720; C++: shorter edge / 720), clamped to 0.4\u20133.0. Audio PulseAudio / ALSA / CoreAudio / DirectSound Qt Multimedia uses the platform's native audio. Network Optional Needed for Spotify, music download and update checks. Local playback, OBD and phone mirroring work offline. Installing a Pre-built Release Every release on the GitHub Releases page is built from the C++ tree by CI. No toolchain, no Python. Platform File Install Windows OCTAVE-<version>-windows-x86_64.exe Run the Inno Setup installer. Bundles Qt, TagLib, FFmpeg DLLs and adb . macOS OCTAVE-<version>-macos.dmg Open, drag to Applications. adb is inside the bundle. Linux x86_64 OCTAVE-<version>-linux-x86_64.AppImage chmod +x and run. Built on Ubuntu 22.04, so it runs on 22.04+ and newer distros. Arch may need fuse2 , or use --appimage-extract-and-run . Linux aarch64 (Pi / Orange Pi) OCTAVE-<version>-linux-aarch64.AppImage Same as above. Install the distro's adb ( sudo apt install adb ) if you want phone mirroring \u2014 Google ships no aarch64 platform-tools. Android OCTAVE-<version>-android-arm64-v8a.apk Sideload. Each release is signed with a fresh keystore, so uninstall the previous version before installing a new one. Python Quick Start The fastest development loop, from zero to a running app: # Clone the repository git clone https://github.com/waybettersolutions/octave.git cd octave # Setup and run (creates venv, installs deps, launches app) python setup.py If you only want to install without launching: python setup.py --no-run To run manually after setup: # Linux / macOS source venv/bin/activate python main.py # Windows venv\\Scripts\\activate python main.py Python is desktop-only The Python backend runs on Linux, Windows, macOS and Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi. It is never packaged (there is no PyInstaller pipeline) and never runs on Android or iOS \u2014 mobile is C++-only. What setup.py Does setup.py The setup script performs these steps in order: Detects your platform \u2014 Windows, macOS, Linux, or Raspberry Pi (by reading /sys/firmware/devicetree/base/model ). On Linux it detects the distro family (Arch, Debian, Fedora) from /etc/os-release , falling back to whichever of pacman , apt , dnf / yum is on PATH . It also notes WSL (\"microsoft\" in /proc/version ). Checks the Python version \u2014 exits with \"Python 3.10+ required\" if below 3.10 (the same floor pyproject.toml declares; see requirements above). Installs system dependencies (Linux only, via sudo ) \u2014 audio (PulseAudio/ALSA), OpenGL/EGL, the XCB platform-plugin libraries, X11, fonts, D-Bus, and on Arch/Fedora the Qt 6 base + multimedia packages. Missing packages are ignored rather than fatal. Creates a virtual environment at ./venv/ with the built-in venv module. An existing venv from the other OS family (a Dropbox-synced Windows venv on Linux, say) or one whose pip no longer runs is removed and recreated. Installs Python dependencies \u2014 upgrades pip , then pip install -r requirements.txt . Launches the application \u2014 unless --no-run is passed (or WSL has no display), it replaces itself with venv/bin/python main.py via os.execv() . Linux (Python backend) Linux is the primary development and deployment platform. The setup script installs the system-level dependencies below through your distro's package manager; they are what PySide6's xcb platform plugin and Qt Multimedia need at runtime. Arch Linux / Manjaro / EndeavourOS System packages installed via pacman : sudo pacman -S --n"}, {"url": "hardware-managers.html", "title": "Hardware Managers", "subtitle": "ESP32 Volume Knob, BerryIMU v3 Sensor Fusion, PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor, and the VolumeController they all feed \u2014 C++ and Python backends side by side", "headings": [], "body": "Hardware Managers - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Hardware Managers Hardware Managers ESP32 Volume Knob, BerryIMU v3 Sensor Fusion, PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor, and the VolumeController they all feed \u2014 C++ and Python backends side by side Overview & Platform Availability Three managers talk to physical peripherals. Each exists twice \u2014 a C++ class in src/managers/ and a PySide6 class in backend/ \u2014 with the same class name, the same QML context-property name and the same public Slots / Signals / Properties. Both are instantiated and registered in src/main.cpp (\"Phase 4: Vehicle & Hardware\") and main.py . Manager QML name C++ Python Log category / logger Transport ESP32VolumeManager esp32VolumeManager src/managers/esp32volumemanager.{h,cpp} backend/esp32_volume_manager.py octave.esp32volume / backend.esp32_volume_manager USB serial (QSerialPort / pyserial) BerryIMUManager berryIMU src/managers/berryimumanager.{h,cpp} backend/berryimu_manager.py octave.berryimu ( octave.imu.mobile on Android) / backend.berryimu_manager I2C bus 2 ( /dev/i2c-2 ) GestureManager gestureSensor src/managers/gesturemanager.{h,cpp} backend/gesture_manager.py octave.gesture / backend.gesture_manager I2C bus 2 ( /dev/i2c-2 ) VolumeController volumeController src/managers/volumecontroller.{h,cpp} backend/volume_utils.py \u2014 \u2014 (routes 0\u2013100 % to every audio output) What runs where Platform ESP32 knob BerryIMU Gesture sensor Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi Full (both backends) Full (both backends) Full (both backends) Windows / macOS Full (both backends) Compiled but inert. C++: startSensor() is wrapped in #ifndef Q_OS_LINUX , logs \"I2C sensors not available on this platform\", emits connectionStatusChanged(\"Disconnected\") and never retries. Python: smbus2 is only installed on Linux ( requirements.txt : smbus2>=0.4.3; sys_platform == \"linux\" ), so the import fails, the manager emits \"Disconnected\" and never retries. Android (C++ only) Stub: never emits, is_connected() is false , detail \"Not available on mobile\" Replaced by a QtSensors implementation ( below ) Stub: connectionStatus = \"Disabled on mobile\", never emits The mobile split is a compile-time switch: every hardware header has an #ifndef Q_OS_MOBILE desktop branch and an #else stub, and CMakeLists.txt defines Q_OS_MOBILE only for Android builds ( $<$<BOOL:${ANDROID}>:Q_OS_MOBILE> ; iOS is noted as \"later\"). The stubs exist so QML Connections handlers and typeof esp32VolumeManager !== \"undefined\" guards resolve on mobile without per-platform QML (see commit 78ab72c ). ESP32 Volume Manager ESP32VolumeManager provides wireless rotary-encoder volume control. Two ESP32 boards are involved: an encoder unit with the knob, and an ESP32-S3 \"receiver dongle\" that plugs into the computer over USB and forwards the encoder's commands as newline-terminated text. OCTAVE only ever talks to the dongle. Linux Windows macOS Firmware is not in this repository The ESP32 sketches are not part of the OCTAVE tree (there are no .ino / PlatformIO sources here). What this page documents is the contract both backends implement: the serial parameters, the incoming command grammar and the outgoing LED commands. Any firmware that speaks this protocol works. ESP-NOW Topology +-------------------+ ESP-NOW +-------------------+ | ESP32 Encoder | ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~> | ESP32-S3 Dongle | | (Rotary Knob) | (Wireless) | (USB Receiver) | | - Rotary encoder | | - USB CDC serial | | - Button press | | - LED ring | +-------------------+ +--------+----------+ | USB Serial 115200 8N1 | +--------v----------+ | OCTAVE | | ESP32VolumeManager| +-------------------+ ESP-NOW is Espressif's connectionless peer-to-peer link; the encoder unit sends + / - / M style commands to the dongle, which forwards them over USB. The link between the two ESP32s is invisible to OCTAVE. Serial Communication Both backends open the port at 115200 baud . C++ sets 8 data bits, no parity, one stop bit, no flow control explicitly ( QSerialPort::Data8 / NoParity / OneStop / NoFlowControl ); Python relies on pyserial's defaults (also 8N1) and adds a 1 s read and write timeout . Incoming bytes are buffered and split on \\n ; each non-empty line is trimmed, upper-cased and handed to the command parser. The two backends read the port very differently: Python C++ Reader Dedicated daemon thread _read_loop , started on connect No thread: QSerialPort::readyRead is delivered on the main event loop and handled by onSerialReadyRead() Waiting readline() blocks for up to the 1 s port timeout (GIL released); a timeout returns b\"\" and the loop re-checks _stop_thread . Before commit 94d61be it polled in_waiting at 100 Hz. Event-driven; readAll() appends to m_readBuffer Partial lines Kept in buffer across reads (a readline() that times out mid-line returns the partial bytes) Kept in m_readBuffer until a \\n arrives Signal delivery volumeChangeRequested / muteToggleRequested are emitted from the read thread (queued to the main thread by Qt) Emitted directly on the main thread def _"}, {"url": "hardware-setup.html", "title": "Hardware Setup", "subtitle": "Wiring, serial protocol, I2C bus, permissions, mounts and first-run verification for the ESP32 volume knob, BerryIMU v3 and PAJ7620U2 gesture sensor.", "headings": [{"id": "platforms", "text": "What Works Where", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32-volume", "text": "ESP32 Wireless Volume Knob", "level": 2}, {"id": "i2c-bus", "text": "I2C Bus 2 &amp; Permissions", "level": 2}, {"id": "berryimu", "text": "BerryIMU v3 IMU Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "gesture-sensor", "text": "PAJ7620U2 Gesture Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "verify", "text": "Verifying the Install (Log Lines)", "level": 2}, {"id": "3d-mounts", "text": "3D Printed Mounts", "level": 2}, {"id": "power", "text": "Power &amp; Vehicle Installation", "level": 2}, {"id": "why", "text": "Why It Is Set Up This Way", "level": 2}], "body": "Hardware Setup - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Hardware Setup Hardware Setup Wiring, serial protocol, I2C bus, permissions, mounts and first-run verification for the ESP32 volume knob, BerryIMU v3 and PAJ7620U2 gesture sensor. What Works Where This page is the physical-side companion to Hardware Managers , which documents the driver code. Both backends (C++ in src/managers/ , Python in backend/ ) implement the same protocols, so the wiring below applies to either. Peripheral Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi Windows / macOS Android ESP32 volume knob (USB serial) Yes Yes No (stub) BerryIMU v3 (I2C) Yes No \u2014 reports \"Disconnected\" and never retries Replaced by the phone's own sensors (QtSensors) PAJ7620U2 gesture sensor (I2C) Yes No \u2014 reports \"Disconnected\" and never retries No (stub, \"Disabled on mobile\") The I2C drivers use Linux /dev/i2c-* devices (Python through smbus2 , which requirements.txt installs only on Linux; C++ through ioctl(I2C_SLAVE) inside #ifdef Q_OS_LINUX ). The reference installation that the timing numbers in the code were measured on is an Orange Pi 5 Plus . ESP32 Wireless Volume Knob Linux Windows macOS Two ESP32 boards: a remote encoder unit (rotary encoder + push button) and an ESP32-S3 receiver dongle plugged into the computer over USB. The encoder talks to the dongle over ESP-NOW; the dongle re-emits the commands as text lines on its USB serial port and drives an LED ring from the replies. OCTAVE only sees the dongle's serial port. Architecture [Rotary Encoder] ESP-NOW (2.4 GHz) [USB Serial] [ + Button ] ----> [ESP32 Dev Board] ~~~~> [ESP32-S3 Dongle] ----> Computer \"Encoder Unit\" \"Receiver\" (OCTAVE) Sends: +, -, M + LED ring 115200 8N1 Forwards to USB Firmware is not in this repository There are no ESP32 sketches under the OCTAVE tree. The table below is the protocol the two OCTAVE backends implement ( backend/esp32_volume_manager.py , src/managers/esp32volumemanager.cpp ); any firmware that speaks it will work. Serial Protocol 115200 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit, no flow control . Every command is one ASCII line terminated by \\n ; OCTAVE trims and upper-cases incoming lines before parsing. Line Direction Meaning + or +N Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Volume up by 1 or N encoder ticks - or -N Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Volume down by 1 or N ticks M Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Mute toggle (button press) P Dongle \u2192 OCTAVE Reserved for play/pause (double-click); accepted and logged, no action V<0-100> OCTAVE \u2192 Dongle Current volume percent for the LED ring (debounced to at most 20/s; also used as the 2 s keepalive) M0 / M1 OCTAVE \u2192 Dongle Mute indicator off / on C<r>,<g>,<b> OCTAVE \u2192 Dongle LED colour, e.g. C255,128,0 Each tick is multiplied by the esp32VolumeStepSize setting (default 1 %, adjustable 0.25\u201310 % in the Volume Knob settings); fractional results accumulate until a whole percent is available, then volumeController.applyVolume() moves every audio output together. Detection and port selection The dongle is recognised by Espressif's USB vendor ID 0x303A (or \"JTAG\" / \"ESP32-S3\" in the port description) and listed first, labelled \" <port> - ESP32-S3 (Recommended) \", in Settings \u2192 Accessories \u2192 Volume Knob . The OBD serial port is never offered. Linux: Python matches the VID via pyserial and adds every /dev/ttyUSB* and /dev/ttyACM* device. macOS: VID match plus /dev/tty.usbserial* , /dev/cu.usbserial* , /dev/tty.usbmodem* , /dev/cu.usbmodem* , /dev/tty.SLAB* , /dev/cu.SLAB* , /dev/tty.wchusbserial* , /dev/cu.wchusbserial* . Windows: all COM ports; the ESP32-S3 by VID or \"USB JTAG/serial debug unit\" in the description. C++ on every platform: QSerialPortInfo::availablePorts() with the same VID / keyword test; every enumerated port is listed. First run: the port is picked for you The esp32VolumePort setting defaults to \"\" (empty) in both backends, and an empty setting means \"auto-select\": on the first start OCTAVE scans, saves the detected ESP32-S3 port and connects. Only override it in the Volume Knob settings if the scan picked the wrong device (OCTAVE saves the choice and reconnects 500 ms later). A settingsConfigure.json written by an older build may still carry the former \"COM7\" default; clear or change it there once. From then on it connects 3 s after every launch. Connection behaviour Startup: a 3 s delay, a port scan, then connect_device() if esp32VolumeEnabled is true. Unplug: detected on read error (Python: any SerialException ; C++: QSerialPort::ResourceError , DeviceNotFoundError , PermissionError , ReadError , WriteError , or a keepalive write the port rejects). With esp32AutoReconnect on, up to 5 attempts at 3, 6, 9, 10, 10 s ( min(10 s, 3 s \u00d7 n) ); the Accessories page shows \"Reconnecting in Ns... (n/5)\" and finally \"Max retries reached. Check connection.\" Pressing Connect there resets the counter. While disconnected, a background scan every 10 s keeps the port list fresh. Changing the port or toggling the feature in settings disconnects / reconnects immediately. LED ring modes Theme ( esp32LedCol"}, {"url": "index.html", "title": "OCTAVE Wiki", "subtitle": "The complete reference for the Open-source Cross-platform Telematics for Augmented Vehicle Experience.\n            Everything you need to understand, use, modify, and extend OCTAVE \u2014 both backends and the shared frontend.", "headings": [], "body": "OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki The complete reference for the Open-source Cross-platform Telematics for Augmented Vehicle Experience. Everything you need to understand, use, modify, and extend OCTAVE \u2014 both backends and the shared frontend. What is OCTAVE? OCTAVE is an open-source (MIT) car head unit / infotainment system you run on your own hardware. It pairs a C++ / Qt 6 backend \u2014 the shipped binary on every platform \u2014 with a parallel Python / PySide6 backend for desktop development and tinkering, both driving a single shared QML frontend . It plays local music and Spotify, reads live data from the car over OBD-II, mirrors an Android phone over USB, shows a live 3D model of the vehicle from an IMU, and recolours its interface to match the album art. It runs on Windows, macOS, Linux, Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi class boards, and Android (iOS is planned but not built yet). The typical installation is a single-board computer behind the dash, a touchscreen, a power source and a 3D-printed mount (the author daily-drives it in a 2003 Jeep TJ on an Orange Pi 5 with a 2560\u00d71080 display). Development and testing can happen on any desktop OS. Core Features \u266b Local Media Player MP3, M4A, FLAC, OGG, Opus and WAV playback with album art carousel, shuffle/repeat, playlists, a persistent metadata store, and album-art colour extraction. \u266c Spotify Integration Spotify Web API with OAuth2 (local callback on port 8888), device switching, playback control and a cached token (OS keyring on Python, file on C++). Desktop only. >_ OBD-II Diagnostics 92 vehicle parameters via an ELM327 adapter (USB / Bluetooth serial; BLE on Android), DTC read and clear, freeze frame, a full-screen shift light, and a protocol transcript in the logs. \u25d4 Gauges & Dashboards Nine gauge primitives, JSON-defined dashboards (five built-in presets plus your own), a chooser with live miniatures, and an in-app drag-and-drop dashboard editor. \u260f Phone Mirror & Android Auto Mirror an Android phone over USB with a built-in scrcpy-protocol client and bundled adb \u2014 nothing to install on the phone \u2014 or run Android Auto through Google's Desktop Head Unit. \u21a7 Music Download Search and download music with Spotify metadata matching and YouTube audio extraction (yt-dlp). Auto-adds to your library. Compiled out for app-store builds. \u2699 Hardware Integrations ESP32 wireless volume knob (ESP-NOW pair, USB dongle, LED ring), BerryIMU v3 9DOF sensor fusion (Madgwick AHRS), PAJ7620U2 gesture recognition. \u26df Live 3D Vehicle A rigged Jeep TJ in Qt Quick 3D with working doors, hood, tailgate, lights, steering and wheels, rolled and pitched in real time by the IMU. \u25c6 Dynamic Theming UI colours adapt in real time to album art. Accent, background and text colours are extracted and applied across every view (and to the ESP32 LED ring). \u223f Waveform Visualizer FFT waveform visualisation with 96 frequency bars, pre-computed off the UI thread and rendered directly in QML. \u270e Diagnostics & Logs Rotating logs from both backends, crash backtraces, a UI-thread watchdog, and a Diagnostics page that copies or exports the logs straight from the dash. \u21bb Network & Updates WiFi status, GitHub update checks and git-based self-update with in-app restart for checkout installs. Platform Support Platform Status Shipped artefact Notes Linux (Debian/Ubuntu/Arch/Fedora) Full support OCTAVE-<v>-linux-x86_64.AppImage Primary development target. All features. Both backends. Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi (Linux aarch64) Full support OCTAVE-<v>-linux-aarch64.AppImage The in-vehicle target. I\u00b2C sensors and the ESP32 knob supported. Google ships no aarch64 adb, so the distro adb is used for phone mirroring. Windows 10+ Full support OCTAVE-<v>-windows-x86_64.exe (Inno Setup) All features except I\u00b2C hardware (BerryIMU, gesture sensor). Python-backend Android Auto capture is Windows-only. macOS (Intel and Apple Silicon) Full support OCTAVE-<v>-macos.dmg All features except I\u00b2C hardware. CI builds on macOS 14 against Qt 6.7.3. Android (arm64-v8a, Android 11+) Sideload OCTAVE-<v>-android-arm64-v8a.apk C++ only. Local media, OBD over BLE, YouTube Music search and download, dashboards, IMU view from the device's own sensors. Spotify, phone mirror, Android Auto, ESP32 and I\u00b2C sensors are excluded. The release APK is signed with a per-build keystore, so updates require an uninstall. iOS Planned, not built \u2014 No CMake toolchain wiring and no CI job yet (see BUILD.md , \"iOS build\"). Wiki Sections Getting Started Installation & First Run \u2014 Pre-built binaries, the Python dev backend, the C++ build, requirements, directory layout Architecture Overview \u2014 Two backends, manager pattern, startup order, threading, volume, video paths, logging, design decisions Backend Deep Dives Settings Manager \u2014 Central settings store, property system, coalesced JSON persistence Media Manager \u2014 Local audio playback, library scanning, album art and colours Spotify Manager \u2014 OAuth2, Web API integration, device control OBD Manager \u2014 ELM327 protocol, 92 vehicle paramet"}, {"url": "media-manager.html", "title": "Media Manager", "subtitle": "Local media playback engine with QMediaPlayer, folder-based playlists, a persistent tag store, cover-art caching, album-art colour theming, volume routing and playback recovery", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "backends", "text": "Backends and Platforms", "level": 2}, {"id": "supported-formats", "text": "Supported Audio Formats", "level": 2}, {"id": "qmediaplayer", "text": "QMediaPlayer Integration", "level": 2}, {"id": "library-scanning", "text": "Library Scanning", "level": 2}, {"id": "metadata-caching", "text": "Metadata Caching and the Tag Store", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art", "text": "Album Art Extraction", "level": 2}, {"id": "color-extraction", "text": "Color Extraction and Theming", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-control", "text": "Playback Control", "level": 2}, {"id": "shuffle-repeat", "text": "Shuffle Logic", "level": 2}, {"id": "playlist-management", "text": "Playlist Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "display-names", "text": "Display Names", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume-mute", "text": "Volume, Mute and Ducking", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-resume", "text": "Playback State Resume", "level": 2}, {"id": "precaching", "text": "Neighbor Pre-caching", "level": 2}, {"id": "error-recovery", "text": "Error Recovery", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Signals", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "threading", "text": "Threading", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "extending", "text": "How to Modify / Extend", "level": 2}], "body": "Media Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Media Manager Media Manager Local media playback engine with QMediaPlayer, folder-based playlists, a persistent tag store, cover-art caching, album-art colour theming, volume routing and playback recovery Overview The MediaManager class ( src/managers/mediamanager.{h,cpp} in the C++ backend, backend/media_manager.py in the Python backend) handles all local audio file playback for OCTAVE. It wraps Qt's QMediaPlayer and QAudioOutput , turns the sub-folders of the music folder into playlists, keeps a metadata and album-art cache backed by a persistent tag store on disk, extracts dominant colors from album art for the \"Album Art Capture\" theme, and provides shuffle, skip, playback resume and automatic recovery from unplayable files. It is registered as a QML context property named mediaManager in both src/main.cpp and main.py . All playback controls are Slots and every state change is broadcast with a Signal, so the QML in frontend/MediaRoom.qml , MediaPlayer.qml , BottomBar.qml , MainMenu.qml and the dashboards/widgets/NowPlayingWidget.qml works identically against either backend. All Platforms Backends and Platforms Implementation Platforms Tag / cover reader src/managers/mediamanager.{h,cpp} All (C++) TagLib on desktop ( TagLib::FileRef for tags, format-specific readers for pictures). On Android the CMake define Q_OS_MOBILE (set for ANDROID builds in CMakeLists.txt ) compiles TagLib out; tags and covers come from OctaveAndroid::readMetadata() / extractAlbumArt() in src/platform/androidmediabridge.h (JNI). Any other mobile target falls back to the file name with \"Unknown Artist\" / \"Unknown Album\". backend/media_manager.py Desktop mutagen ( MP3 / ID3 for MP3, mutagen.mp4.MP4 for M4A/MP4/AAC, FLAC / OggVorbis / OggOpus for the Xiph containers, mutagen.wave.WAVE for WAV, generic mutagen.File for duration only on anything else) Both classes expose the same slot, signal and JSON surface. Where the two implementations differ in behavior this page says so explicitly; everything else applies to both. Supported Audio Formats The following file extensions are recognized during library scanning ( MediaManager::s_audioExtensions in C++, AUDIO_EXTENSIONS in Python): // C++ (src/managers/mediamanager.cpp) \".mp3\" , \".m4a\" , \".mp4\" , \".flac\" , \".ogg\" , \".opus\" , \".wav\" # Python (backend/media_manager.py) AUDIO_EXTENSIONS = ( '.mp3' , '.m4a' , '.mp4' , '.flac' , '.ogg' , '.opus' , '.wav' ) Both lists accept .mp4 : YouTube serves audio-only streams in an MP4 container that yt-dlp names with a literal .mp4 extension, and those files are the same format as .m4a . The Python module also exposes strip_audio_extension(filename) , which removes any of these extensions (and only these) and is what the display-name and metadata fallbacks use. Which tags and embedded covers each backend can read is the same for every format except WAV art, which neither backend extracts. Python's dispatch lives in _read_tags(file_path, base_name) (tags, never raises) and _extract_art_for(file_path, album_id) (cover, mirrors the C++ function of the same name); a file that fails to open yields the file-name title with \"Unknown Artist\" / \"Unknown Album\" in both backends: Format C++ (TagLib, desktop) Python (mutagen) MP3 Title / artist / album / length via FileRef ; cover from the first ID3v2 APIC frame Title / artist / album via ID3 ( TIT2 , TPE1 , TALB ), length via MP3 ; cover from the first APIC frame M4A / MP4 / AAC Tags via FileRef ; cover from the first covr item ( TagLib::MP4::File ) Tags \\xa9nam , \\xa9ART , \\xa9alb ; cover from the first covr item ( _extract_album_art_mp4 ) FLAC Tags via FileRef ; cover from TagLib::FLAC::File::pictureList() Vorbis comments title , artist , album via mutagen.flac.FLAC ; cover from the first PICTURE block ( FLAC.pictures , _extract_album_art_flac ) Ogg Vorbis Tags via FileRef ; cover from the Xiph comment's pictureList() ( TagLib::Ogg::Vorbis::File ) Vorbis comments via mutagen.oggvorbis.OggVorbis ; cover from the base64 FLAC picture block in the METADATA_BLOCK_PICTURE comment ( _extract_album_art_ogg ) Opus Tags via FileRef ; cover from the Xiph comment's pictureList() . _extract_album_art_ogg picks the reader by extension -- TagLib::Ogg::Opus::File for .opus , since the Vorbis reader rejects an Opus stream -- and shares the picture handling Vorbis comments via mutagen.oggopus.OggOpus ; cover from METADATA_BLOCK_PICTURE , as for Ogg Vorbis WAV Tags and length via FileRef ; no cover Tags from the optional ID3 chunk via mutagen.wave.WAVE ( TIT2 , TPE1 , TALB ); no cover QMediaPlayer Integration The constructor creates one QMediaPlayer with one QAudioOutput and starts it at volume 0.5 (the real startup volume is applied a moment later by VolumeController , see Volume ). The Python constructor additionally sets the probe-size (10 MB) and analyzeduration (5 s) dynamic properties on the player for more reliable format probing; the C++ constructor does not. // src/managers/mediam"}, {"url": "network-manager.html", "title": "Network Manager", "subtitle": "Connectivity polling with offline backoff, ancestry-aware GitHub update checks, and git-based self-update for source checkouts", "headings": [], "body": "Network Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Network Manager Network Manager Connectivity polling with offline backoff, ancestry-aware GitHub update checks, and git-based self-update for source checkouts Overview The NetworkManager has three jobs: report whether the head unit is online (and on which network), tell the user when the main branch on GitHub has moved past the running commit, and -- when OCTAVE runs from a git checkout -- pull that update in place and restart. It is defined in src/managers/networkmanager.{h,cpp} (C++) and backend/network_manager.py (Python) and registered as the QML context property networkManager in src/main.cpp / main.py . Both backends expose the same properties, signals, slots and status strings; where the mechanics differ the sections below say so. Thread Safety In the Python backend every blocking operation (subprocess calls, HTTP requests) runs on a ThreadPoolExecutor with a single worker, and results are polled back on the main thread by a 250 ms QTimer so Qt signals are only ever emitted from the GUI thread. The C++ backend has no worker thread: it uses asynchronous QProcess and QNetworkAccessManager whose completion slots already run on the main thread. The only synchronous calls in C++ are the short git queries ( runGitSync() : status --porcelain , cat-file , merge-base , rev-list , and the rollback reset --hard ) -- the header comment says they run on the GUI thread \"deliberately: they gate a destructive action, so their answer must be current at the moment of the click\". The manager targets the GitHub repository WayBetterSolutions/OCTAVE ( GITHUB_REPO ) and compares the local git HEAD against the tip of the remote main branch. Architecture Python: NetworkManager (backend/network_manager.py) | |-- ThreadPoolExecutor (1 worker) -- one job at a time (_pending_future) | |-- _do_network_check() nmcli/networksetup/netsh + generate_204 | |-- _do_update_check_sync() GitHub API + git ancestry queries | |-- _do_self_update_sync() fetch / reset / py_compile / pip / log | |-- QTimer _poll_timer 30 s online, 120 s after 3 misses |-- QTimer _result_poll_timer 250 ms: future done? progress message? |-- QTimer.singleShot(2000) _startup_probe: git --version + first check | |-- Signals --> QML (all emitted from the main thread) C++: NetworkManager (src/managers/networkmanager.cpp) | |-- QNetworkAccessManager m_nam generate_204 probe, GitHub API (async replies) |-- QProcess m_networkNameProcess nmcli / networksetup / netsh (async) |-- QProcess m_gitProcess self-update step machine (async, one step at a time) |-- runGitSync() short blocking git queries that gate the reset | |-- QTimer m_pollTimer kPollOnlineMs 30000 / kPollOfflineMs 120000 |-- QTimer m_resultPollTimer 250 ms progress relay (see Self-Update) |-- QTimer::singleShot(2000) checkCanSelfUpdate() + refreshNetworkStatus() | |-- busy flags: m_networkCheckBusy, m_updateCheckBusy, m_updating Background Work Pattern (Python) Only one background job runs at a time. _submit_work() takes a callable and a result-type tag, submits it to the executor, and starts the result poll timer: def _submit_work ( self , fn, result_type): \"\"\"Submit blocking work to the thread pool. Only one job at a time.\"\"\" if self ._pending_future and not self ._pending_future.done(): return # Already busy self ._pending_future = self ._executor.submit(fn) self ._pending_future._result_type = result_type self ._result_poll_timer.start() Each tick of _check_pending_result() first relays any pending progress message, then -- if the future is done -- stops the timer, unwraps the result and routes it by tag: \"network_status\" , \"update_check\" or \"self_update\" . An exception from the job maps to updateStatus = \"error\" (\"Could not reach GitHub\") or selfUpdateStatus = \"error\" (\"Update failed unexpectedly\"). A consequence of the single slot: a network poll that lands while a self-update is running is silently skipped. The C++ port instead keeps one busy flag per activity ( m_networkCheckBusy , m_updateCheckBusy , m_updating ), so a connectivity probe and an update check can be in flight at the same time. Connectivity Detection Network status is polled every 30 seconds while online. After three consecutive failed probes the interval backs off to 2 minutes and returns to 30 s on the first success ( POLL_ONLINE_MS / POLL_OFFLINE_MS / OFFLINE_STREAK_FOR_BACKOFF in Python, kPollOnlineMs / kPollOfflineMs / kOfflineStreakForBackoff in C++). The first probe runs 2 s after startup so the UI can paint first; the git --version probe behind canSelfUpdate runs at that same moment rather than in the constructor. Python runs the name lookup and the internet probe sequentially inside one worker job; C++ starts them in parallel. Network Name Detection _get_network_name_sync() (Python) / startNetworkNameCheck() + onNetworkNameCheckFinished() (C++) shell out to the platform's network tool. The WiFi command runs first; if it yields no SSID a second command looks for a wired link and reports "}, {"url": "obd-manager.html", "title": "OBD Manager", "subtitle": "OBD-II vehicle diagnostics: a threaded ELM327 connection worker, 83 pollable parameters on both backends, DTC reading, an Android BLE bridge, and the parameter model that feeds every gauge", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "architecture", "text": "Architecture and Files", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-consumers", "text": "QML Consumers", "level": 2}, {"id": "connection-flow", "text": "Connection Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "threaded-worker", "text": "Threaded Connection Worker", "level": 2}, {"id": "elm-init", "text": "ELM327 Init Sequence and Vehicle Probe", "level": 2}, {"id": "poll-loop", "text": "Poll Loop and Pacing (C++)", "level": 2}, {"id": "port-lifecycle", "text": "Port Lifecycle: Never Leave a Dead Port Open", "level": 2}, {"id": "speed-optimizations", "text": "Speed Optimizations", "level": 2}, {"id": "device-discovery", "text": "Device Discovery", "level": 2}, {"id": "unified-adapter-api", "text": "Unified Adapter API", "level": 2}, {"id": "auto-reconnect", "text": "Auto-Reconnect and Passive Scanning", "level": 2}, {"id": "data-watchdog", "text": "Data Watchdog", "level": 2}, {"id": "supported-parameters", "text": "Supported OBD Parameters", "level": 2}, {"id": "watcher-system", "text": "Which PIDs Get Polled", "level": 2}, {"id": "vehicle-scanning", "text": "Vehicle Scanning", "level": 2}, {"id": "diagnostics", "text": "Diagnostics (DTC)", "level": 2}, {"id": "android-ble", "text": "Android: BLE via OctaveOBDBridge", "level": 2}, {"id": "elm327-protocol", "text": "ELM327 Protocol Module", "level": 2}, {"id": "protocol-logging", "text": "Protocol Logging", "level": 2}, {"id": "parameter-model", "text": "OBDParameterModel and IMU Parameters", "level": 2}, {"id": "shift-light", "text": "Shift Light, Full-Screen Flash and Aura", "level": 2}, {"id": "demo-mode", "text": "Demo and Simulated Data", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-keys", "text": "Settings Keys", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Signals and Properties", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "OBD Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / OBD Manager OBD Manager OBD-II vehicle diagnostics: a threaded ELM327 connection worker, 83 pollable parameters on both backends, DTC reading, an Android BLE bridge, and the parameter model that feeds every gauge Overview OBDManager provides real-time vehicle data over the OBD-II port through an ELM327-compatible adapter. It exists in both backends and is registered as the QML context property obdManager : backend/obd_manager.py (Python, desktop only) drives the adapter through the python-obd library: obd.Async() owns the serial port, does protocol negotiation, and calls back into the manager for every watched PID. src/managers/obdmanager.{h,cpp} (C++, every shipped binary) talks ELM327 directly: on desktop an OBDConnectionWorker owns a QSerialPort on its own thread; on Android the same class switches (behind #ifdef Q_OS_ANDROID ) to a BLE GATT link provided by the Java helper android/src/org/octave/app/OctaveOBDBridge.java . Both expose the same signals, slots and properties, so the shared QML ( OBDMenu , OBDHome , OBDDiagnostics , HomeOBDView , the OBD settings page and the gauge/dashboard system ) never knows which one is running. Where the two backends genuinely differ, this page says so explicitly. All Platforms Python: desktop only Android: C++ + Java BLE bridge Architecture and Files File Platform Description backend/obd_manager.py Desktop OBDManager + OBDConnectionWorker built on python-obd ( obd.Async for live data, obd.OBD sync connections for DTC work). 83 obd.commands.* entries in _get_all_commands() . backend/elm327_protocol.py Desktop (reference) Pure-Python ELM327 protocol: init sequence, the 83-entry PID table with decoders and python-obd command names, parse_elm_voltage() , supported_command_names() , response/DTC parsing, ResponseBuffer . Its docstring now states what it is: the parity twin of the C++ ELM327Protocol , not imported by any live Python manager (python-obd does the I/O and decoding on the Python side), kept so the PID table and decoders can be diffed and exercised in plain Python. src/managers/obdmanager.{h,cpp} All (C++) OBDManager (main thread: timers, settings, signal dispatch table) and OBDConnectionWorker (worker thread: QSerialPort , init, polling, DTC, scan). The Android BLE state machine lives at the bottom of the .cpp under #ifdef Q_OS_ANDROID . src/managers/elm327protocol.{h,cpp} All (C++) Static ELM327Protocol class (init commands, PID table with a commandName column, parsers, elmVoltageRequest() / parseElmVoltage() , supportedCommandNames() , defaultPids() ), ResponseBuffer , and the octave.elm327 logging category. android/src/org/octave/app/OctaveOBDBridge.java Android Static Java class wrapping BluetoothGatt . Called from C++ via QJniObject : connect , disconnect , write , stateCode , pollResponse , pollLog , lastError , lastEvent , bondedDevices (paired adapters as \"MAC|name\" ). frontend/OBDParameterModel.qml QML Singleton that maps every *Changed signal to a paramId and holds the live paramValues map every gauge binds to (see below ). dev/mock_obd_manager.py Dev mode MockOBDManager : simulated ELM327 with the same signal surface, installed instead of the real manager by dev/dev_runtime.py ( python -m dev.main_dev ). Desktop (Python) Desktop (C++) Android (C++) +-------------------+ +-------------------+ +-------------------+ | OBDManager | | OBDManager | | OBDManager | | (main thread) | | (main thread) | | (main thread, | +---------+---------+ +---------+---------+ | Q_OS_ANDROID) | | QThread | QThread \"obd-worker\" +---------+---------+ +---------+---------+ +---------+---------+ | QJniObject | OBDConnectionWorker| | OBDConnectionWorker| +---------+---------+ | obd.Async(port) | | QSerialPort 38400 | | OctaveOBDBridge | | python-obd polls | | ELM327Protocol | | BluetoothGatt | +---------+---------+ +---------+---------+ | ISSC/FFF0/FFE0 | | | +---------+---------+ /dev/rfcomm0, ttyUSB*, same ports (rfcomm bind | BLE notify COM*, /dev/tty.* done by the manager on Linux) | | | | +---------+---------------------------------------------------------+--------+---------+ | ELM327 adapter -> OBD-II port -> vehicle ECU | +--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ QML Consumers QML file What it uses frontend/OBDParameterModel.qml Connects to all 84 parameter signals and mirrors them into paramValues . Every gauge, the parameter-card grid and the home cards read from here, never from obdManager directly. frontend/OBDMenu.qml Parameter-card grid (one card per enabled parameter, filtered with settingsManager.get_obd_parameter_enabled ), the dashboards chooser/renderer, the RPM/shift-light settings popup. frontend/OBDHome.qml Status row bound to connectionStatusChanged / connectionStatusDetailChanged , a 40-line rolling log fed by connectionLogLineAppended , and the sub-page buttons. frontend/OBDDiagnostics.qml enter_diagnostic_mode() on creation and on every visible transition, exit_diagno"}, {"url": "pages.html", "title": "Pages", "subtitle": "Every view that can be pushed on the StackView (plus the window-level Now Playing Studio): what it shows, how it is reached, which managers it talks to, and the QML techniques worth knowing", "headings": [{"id": "inventory", "text": "View Inventory", "level": 2}, {"id": "mainmenu", "text": "MainMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaroom", "text": "MediaRoom.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaplayer", "text": "MediaPlayer.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "downloadpage", "text": "DownloadPage.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdhome", "text": "OBDHome.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdmenu", "text": "OBDMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "obddiagnostics", "text": "OBDDiagnostics.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensorhome", "text": "SensorHome.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "sensormenu", "text": "SensorMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "carmenu", "text": "CarMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "clockmenu", "text": "ClockMenu.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "phonemirror", "text": "PhoneMirrorView.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "androidauto", "text": "AndroidAutoView.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "settingsmenu", "text": "SettingsMenu and its Layouts", "level": 2}, {"id": "nowplayingstudio", "text": "NowPlayingStudio.qml", "level": 2}, {"id": "navigation-summary", "text": "Navigation Summary", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Pages - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Pages Pages Every view that can be pushed on the StackView (plus the window-level Now Playing Studio): what it shows, how it is reached, which managers it talks to, and the QML techniques worth knowing View Inventory All views live at the top of frontend/ and are instantiated with Qt.createComponent() when navigated to (see Page Routing ). The table lists what each one declares and where it is pushed from; \"required\" means required property , so a createObject() call that omits it returns null . View objectName Properties it takes Reached from MainMenu mainMenu stackView , mainWindow , windowWidth , windowHeight (plain) StackView.initialItem ; BottomBar Home pops back to it MediaRoom mediaRoom stackView , mainWindow (plain) BottomBar Media, MainMenu media panel, MediaPlayer row tap, DownloadPage play MediaPlayer mediaPlayer stackView , mainWindow (required) BottomBar Media (toggles with MediaRoom), MainMenu media panel DownloadPage downloadPage stackView (plain), mainWindow (plain) openDownloadPage() in MediaRoom and MediaPlayer (cached instance) OBDHome obdHome stackView , mainWindow (required) BottomBar OBD OBDMenu obdMenu stackView , mainWindow (required) OBDHome \"Dashboards\" card, MainMenu OBD panel, BottomBar OBD via lastOBDPage OBDDiagnostics obdDiagnostics stackView , mainWindow (required) OBDHome \"Trouble Codes\" card, BottomBar OBD via lastOBDPage SensorHome sensorHome stackView , mainWindow (required) BottomBar Sensor button and the BottomBar clock SensorMenu sensorMenu stackView , mainWindow (required) SensorHome \"Sensors\" card, BottomBar Sensor via lastSensorPage CarMenu carMenu stackView (required), mainWindow (plain) SensorHome \"3D View\" card, BottomBar Sensor via lastSensorPage ClockMenu \u2014 stackView , mainWindow (required) only navigateTo(\"ClockMenu.qml\") (command server / screenshot sweeper); no in-app button PhoneMirrorView phoneMirrorView (set by BottomBar at createObject ) stackView , mainWindow (plain) BottomBar Phone Mirror (desktop only) AndroidAutoView \u2014 stackView , mainWindow (plain) BottomBar Android Auto (desktop only) SettingsMenu settingsMenu stackView , mainWindow , initialSection (required) BottomBar Settings, navigateToSettings() DashboardEditor ( dashboards/editor/ ) \u2014 stackView , callerPage , optional editingId / draftJson OBDMenu chooser (+ New, Edit, \u25b2 Draft); see Gauges & Dashboards Every view uses the OBD or media colour tokens from Style and the scaled sizes from Spacing , and every one carries the local dp() / dpMin() shim described in GUI Scale . MainMenu.qml frontend/MainMenu.qml (563 lines) is the home screen and the initialItem of the StackView: \"Media on left (1/3), OBD on right (2/3)\". It imports QtQuick.Controls.Basic , Qt5Compat.GraphicalEffects and QtQuick.Effects \u2014 the only file besides MediaRoom that uses both effect modules. Layout RowLayout { anchors.fill : parent; anchors.margins : dp ( 10 ); spacing : dp ( 10 ) // LEFT: media (Layout.preferredWidth: parent.width * 0.33, clip: true) Rectangle { id : mediaSection ... } // RIGHT: OBD (Layout.fillWidth: true, 2 px accent border) Rectangle { HomeOBDView { anchors.fill : parent; anchors.margins : dp ( 5 ) } } } Media panel Blurred album art background \u2014 an Image of the current art with layer.effect: MultiEffect { blurEnabled: true; blurMax: 64; blur: min(1, settingsManager.backgroundBlurRadius / 64) } under a #C0000000 overlay; the panel border is drawn by a separate border-only rectangle at z: 10 so it stays crisp above the blur. Foreground album art with the file's single DropShadow (offset 4/4, radius 12, 25 samples). Scrolling title and \"artist \u2022 album\" \u2014 two copies of the Flickable + 3 s Timer + 5 s InOutQuad NumberAnimation ping-pong on contentX , running only while the text is wider than its container. Seek slider \u2014 a custom Slider (accent fill, round handle, userSeeking flag) that on release calls spotifyManager.set_position() when useSpotify is true and mediaManager.set_position() otherwise, flanked by position/duration labels. Dual source \u2014 useSpotify is settingsManager.mediaSource === \"spotify\" && spotifyManager.is_connected() ; currentArt/Title/Artist/Album pick Spotify values or private _local* copies (\"to avoid binding loops\"). Missing art falls back to assets/missing_art.png . Tap to open the player \u2014 a TapHandler , not a MouseArea , pushes MediaPlayer.qml or MediaRoom.qml according to settingsManager.musicButtonDefaultPage . The comment explains: the layout is full of a Flickable title, a Flickable metadata row and a Slider, \"all of which consume MouseArea clicks. TapHandler observes taps without interfering with those drag interactions\". OBD panel Hosts HomeOBDView ; a MouseArea over the whole panel pushes \"OBDMenu.qml\" by string with { stackView, mainWindow } \u2014 straight to the gauges, bypassing OBDHome. Component.onCompleted and onHomeOBDParametersChanged call obdManager.refresh_values() so the cards are populated immediately. Backend managers used mediaManage"}, {"url": "phone-mirror-android-auto.html", "title": "Phone Mirror &amp; Android Auto", "subtitle": "OCTAVE's built-in scrcpy-protocol client with its forked phone server, and the Google DHU-based Android Auto path", "headings": [], "body": "Phone Mirror & Android Auto - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Phone Mirror & Android Auto Phone Mirror & Android Auto OCTAVE's built-in scrcpy-protocol client with its forked phone server, and the Google DHU-based Android Auto path Overview OCTAVE has two ways of putting a connected Android phone on the dash: Phone Mirror -- OCTAVE is its own scrcpy-protocol client. It pushes OCTAVE's forked scrcpy server to the phone over adb , decodes the H.264 stream itself, plays the phone's audio, and injects real multitouch over the control socket. Nothing has to be installed by the user. Both backends implement it in full; it is desktop-only (the Android build registers a stub). Android Auto -- Runs Google's Desktop Head Unit (DHU) as a separate process and embeds its window. In practice this works only with the Python backend on Windows; see What actually works . The two paths share one thing: adb , found once by PhoneMirrorManager::findAdb() / _find_adb() and reused by Android Auto. Phone Mirror pushes decoded frames into a QVideoSink bound to a QML VideoOutput ; Android Auto embeds a native window (Python/Windows) and additionally registers a QQuickImageProvider ( image://dhuframe/ ) that no QML currently reads. Desktop only +-------------------------------+ USB / adb +--------------------------------------+ | Android phone | <-----------> | OCTAVE (C++ or Python backend) | | | | | | octave-phone-server (jar) | video socket | ScrcpyClient | | org.octave.phoneserver | -------------> | demux -> libavcodec / PyAV | | virtual display 1280x800 | audio socket | -> QVideoFrame -> QVideoSink | | MediaCodec H.264 encoder | -------------> | PCM -> gain -> QAudioSink | | MirrorKeeper (sleep/panel) | control socket | touch / keys / keeper policy | | | <------------> | PHONE_STATE device messages | | | | | | | Google DHU (Android Auto) | tcp:5277 | PhoneMirrorManager -> PhoneMirrorView| | head unit server on phone | <-----------> | AndroidAutoManager -> AndroidAutoView| +-------------------------------+ +--------------------------------------+ Phone Mirror Phone Mirror shows an Android phone inside OCTAVE and lets the user drive it from the dash with real multitouch. OCTAVE is a scrcpy-protocol client : it pushes the bundled phone server jar ( tools/phone-server/octave-phone-server ) to /data/local/tmp/octave-phone-server.jar with adb , starts it with app_process , decodes the H.264 stream and injects input over the control socket. The server is OCTAVE's fork of the scrcpy server ( phone_server/ , Apache-2.0, forked at v3.3.4, Java package org.octave.phoneserver , version string 3.3.4-octave ). It needs shell privileges, which only an adb-launched process gets, so it cannot be an installed app. There is no scrcpy binary, no v4l2loopback, no ffmpeg subprocess and no adb shell input anywhere in the path (all removed in commit 7a8c8b8 ). File Class Role src/managers/phonemirrormanager.{h,cpp} / backend/phone_mirror/manager.py PhoneMirrorManager adb discovery, device probes, settings, session lifecycle, keeper policy, ducking; QML context property phoneMirrorManager src/phone_mirror/scrcpyclient.{h,cpp} / backend/phone_mirror/scrcpy_client.py ScrcpyClient One session: server push/launch or attach, sockets, demux, decode, black-frame hold, audio playback, control and device messages phone_server/src/main/java/org/octave/phoneserver/ Server , DesktopConnection , Streamer , Controller , MirrorKeeper , NewDisplayCapture , CleanUp The program running on the phone frontend/PhoneMirrorView.qml -- VideoOutput + MultiPointTouchArea + nav row, reconnect and asleep overlays, error/retry screen frontend/settings/AccessoriesSettingsPage.qml -- Phone mirror settings card (display size, phone screen, audio, gain, ducking, status line) Logging: C++ categories octave.phonemirror (manager) and octave.phonemirror.client ; Python loggers backend.phone_mirror.manager and backend.phone_mirror.scrcpy_client . Server log lines are relayed at INFO (WARN/ERROR lines at warning level) with a server: prefix. Measured on the Orange Pi 5 Plus with a Galaxy S22 Ultra (Android 16), from the click on the button: handshake at +0.34 s (Python) / +0.51 s (C++), first decoded frame at +0.65\u20130.83 s regardless of how long the phone has been idle, a tap visible in the stream after ~0.4 s, 23\u201328 % of one core while scrolling in Python and the same order in C++. Numbers and history: docs/PHONE_MIRROR_NATIVE_PLAN.md . PhoneMirrorManager Owns the single session (one phone at a time), the adb probes the UI needs, and the settings that shape a session. Identical public surface in both backends; the mobile C++ build compiles a stub with the same members that reports nativeAvailable = false and \"Phone mirroring is desktop-only\". Properties Property Type Description adbPath str adb in use. Search order: bundled platform-tools (C++: <app dir>/platform-tools/ , <app dir>/../tools/platform-tools/<os>/ , <app dir>/../Resources/platform-tools/ ; Python: <repo>/tools/platform-tools/<os>/ , "}, {"url": "settings-manager.html", "title": "Settings Manager", "subtitle": "Central settings store: one JSON file, an in-memory cache served to QML through Qt properties, a coalesced atomic disk write, and a metadata registry that drives the Quick Panel", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "file-location", "text": "File Location and Storage Paths", "level": 2}, {"id": "property-signal-system", "text": "Property / Signal / Slot System", "level": 2}, {"id": "lifecycle", "text": "Startup, In-Memory Cache and Coalesced Writes", "level": 2}, {"id": "json-persistence", "text": "JSON Persistence and Validation", "level": 2}, {"id": "settings-registry", "text": "Settings Registry Metadata System", "level": 2}, {"id": "default-settings", "text": "Default Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "key-methods", "text": "Key Methods and Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "section-visibility", "text": "Section Visibility System", "level": 2}, {"id": "pinned-settings", "text": "Pinned Quick Settings", "level": 2}, {"id": "generic-access", "text": "Generic Setting Access", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-colors", "text": "Album Art Colors", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-connection", "text": "Connection to QML and Other Managers", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-new-setting", "text": "How to Add a New Setting", "level": 2}, {"id": "signal-reference", "text": "Signal Reference", "level": 2}, {"id": "parity-notes", "text": "Backend Parity Notes", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings Manager Settings Manager Central settings store: one JSON file, an in-memory cache served to QML through Qt properties, a coalesced atomic disk write, and a metadata registry that drives the Quick Panel Overview The SettingsManager class ( src/managers/settingsmanager.{h,cpp} in C++, backend/settings_manager.py in Python) is the central configuration store for all of OCTAVE. It inherits from QObject and exposes every user-configurable option to QML through Q_PROPERTY / @Property getters, change signals, and save_* slots. Both backends define the same 92 default keys, the same 75 properties, the same 79 signals and the same slot names, and they read and write the same file: settingsConfigure.json in the OS-specific app data directory. The QML frontend never knows which backend is behind settingsManager . Beyond the typed properties, the manager provides a settings registry (25 entries) that maps a setting ID to its label, category, control type and save slot. The Quick Panel and the pin system render settings from this metadata, so a slider or toggle can be pinned without any per-setting QML. Since the 2026-09 performance sweep the manager is memory-served : the file is read once in the constructor, every getter answers from the in-memory copy, and every setter updates that copy and arms a 500 ms single-shot timer that performs one atomic write for the whole burst. Details in Startup, In-Memory Cache and Coalesced Writes . All Platforms File Location and Storage Paths The settings file lives in the directory returned by get_app_data_dir() (Python, module-level function) / SettingsManager::getAppDataDir() (C++, static). Both create the directory if it is missing and pick the path by platform: Platform Path Rule in code Windows %APPDATA%\\OCTAVE\\settingsConfigure.json APPDATA env var; falls back to the home directory if unset macOS ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json hard-coded under the home directory Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi ~/.config/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE when the variable is set Android / iOS (C++ only) <QDir::homePath()>/.config/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json same #else branch as Linux ( XDG_CONFIG_HOME honoured if set) def get_app_data_dir (): app_name = \"OCTAVE\" if sys.platform == 'win32' : base = os.environ.get( 'APPDATA' , os.path.expanduser( '~' )) data_dir = os.path.join(base, app_name) elif sys.platform == 'darwin' : data_dir = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser( '~' ), 'Library' , 'Application Support' , app_name) else : base = os.environ.get( 'XDG_CONFIG_HOME' , os.path.join(os.path.expanduser( '~' ), '.config' )) data_dir = os.path.join(base, app_name) os.makedirs(data_dir, exist_ok= True ) return data_dir The same directory is the root for everything else OCTAVE persists, so the helper is imported by other managers rather than duplicated: Path under the app data dir Owner settingsConfigure.json SettingsManager (this page) theme_art/theme_<safe name>.<ext> SettingsManager, album art copied or downloaded for a custom theme ( save_custom_theme ) logs/ src/util/logger.cpp , backend/diagnostics_manager.py / src/managers/diagnosticsmanager.cpp (see Development ) dashboards/ DashboardManager user dashboards ( Gauges & Dashboards ) metadata_cache.json , display_names.json MediaManager .spotify_token_cache SpotifyManager (C++ token cache; the Python backend prefers the OS keyring) Spotify credentials are stored in this file spotifyClientId and spotifyClientSecret are plain strings inside settingsConfigure.json . That is why the file is written with chmod 600 on Unix. Only the OAuth token goes to the keyring (Python) or .spotify_token_cache (C++), never the client credentials. SettingsManager itself never touches the keyring. Property / Signal / Slot System Every setting with a dedicated property follows the same three-part pattern in both languages: Signal declaration named <key>Changed (e.g. themeSettingChanged ), carrying the new value where the value is scalar and no argument for dict/list settings Read-only property with the camelCase JSON key as its name and the signal as notify Save slot named save_<snake_case> that updates the backing member, calls update_setting() / updateSetting() , and emits the signal Python ( backend/settings_manager.py ): # 1. Signal declaration (class level) themeSettingChanged = Signal( str ) # 2. Property with notify signal @Property( str , notify=themeSettingChanged) def themeSetting (self): return self._theme_setting # 3. Slot for QML to call @Slot( str ) def save_theme_setting (self, theme): logger.debug(f \"Saving theme setting: {theme}\" ) self._theme_setting = theme self.update_setting( \"themeSetting\" , theme, self.themeSettingChanged) C++ ( src/managers/settingsmanager.{h,cpp} ), same three parts: // header Q_PROPERTY(QString themeSetting READ themeSetting NOTIFY themeSettingChanged) signals : void themeSettingChanged ( const QString &value); public slots"}, {"url": "settings-reference.html", "title": "Settings Reference", "subtitle": "Every key in settingsConfigure.json for both backends: type, default, allowed values, the UI page that owns it, and what it affects.", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "device", "text": "Device and Window", "level": 2}, {"id": "display", "text": "Display", "level": 2}, {"id": "media", "text": "Media and Now Playing", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-resume", "text": "Playback Resume", "level": 2}, {"id": "spotify", "text": "Spotify", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd", "text": "OBD", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd-parameters", "text": "OBD Parameter PIDs", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32", "text": "ESP32 Volume Knob", "level": 2}, {"id": "imu", "text": "IMU Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "gesture", "text": "Gesture Sensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone", "text": "Android Auto and Phone Mirror", "level": 2}, {"id": "download", "text": "Downloads", "level": 2}, {"id": "ui-state", "text": "Settings UI State", "level": 2}, {"id": "generic", "text": "QML-Only Keys (no backend default)", "level": 2}, {"id": "in-memory", "text": "In-Memory Only", "level": 2}, {"id": "persistence", "text": "Persistence and File Format", "level": 2}, {"id": "parity", "text": "C++ / Python Parity Check", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings Reference - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings Reference Settings Reference Every key in settingsConfigure.json for both backends: type, default, allowed values, the UI page that owns it, and what it affects. Overview Settings are owned by SettingsManager ( src/managers/settingsmanager.{h,cpp} in the shipped C++ build, backend/settings_manager.py in the Python developer backend) and persisted to settingsConfigure.json in the OS-specific config directory: Windows: %APPDATA%/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json macOS: ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json Linux / Raspberry Pi / Orange Pi: ~/.config/OCTAVE/settingsConfigure.json (or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE/ ) Android / iOS (C++ only): the Linux rule applied to QDir::homePath() Both backends define the same 92 top-level default keys with identical literal defaults (verified key by key against both sources on 2026-09-10). Settings are read once at startup, validated against the defaults, served from memory, and written back atomically 500 ms after the last change. The mechanics are on the Settings Manager page; this page is the key catalogue. How to read the tables: Property : the QML-visible property name (same as the JSON key) or how QML reaches a key that has no property. Type : JSON type; the C++ property type is noted where it differs from the Python one. Default : the literal in both backends' default dicts. Range / Values : what the save slot enforces (clamp or whitelist), and in parentheses the range the UI control offers when it is narrower. A dash means the slot stores whatever it is given. UI : the page under frontend/settings/ (Display, Media, OBD, Accessories, Device, About) or the other QML file that writes the key. See Settings UI . Pinnable settings 25 of these keys are also registered in SETTINGS_REGISTRY and can be pinned to the Quick Panel. They are marked pinnable in the description; the registry itself is documented on the Settings Manager page. Device and Window Property JSON Key Type Default Range / Values UI Description deviceName deviceName str \"Default Device\" \u2013 Device page (via Main.updateDeviceName() , which rejects blank names) Display name for this OCTAVE installation fontSetting fontSetting str \"GoogleSans Regular\" \u2013 Display page UI font family; Style.setFont() is called on change. The default is the display name Main.qml derives from the bundled GoogleSans-Regular.ttf ; a stored legacy \"Google Sans\" (which Style.setFont() rejected, leaving the system font) is migrated to it at load and written back windowState windowState str \"windowed\" \"windowed\", \"fullscreen\", \"maximized\" (others ignored) Display page (Window) Window mode applied at startup screenWidth screenWidth int 1280 \u2013 Display page; Main.qml also saves the size on window resize Window width in pixels when windowed screenHeight screenHeight int 720 \u2013 Display page; Main.qml on resize Window height in pixels when windowed Display Property JSON Key Type Default Range / Values UI Description uiScale uiScale float (C++ float ) 1.0 \u2013 (UI 0.5 \u2013 2.0, step 0.05) Display page Global UI scale factor ( Spacing.globalScale ). A stored 0.6 (an old default) is migrated to 1.0 at load. Pinnable themeSetting themeSetting str \"CosmicVoyager\" Any built-in or custom theme name; \"Album Art Capture\" enables palette extraction Display page; Main.updateTheme() Active colour theme ( Theme System ). Pinnable (chips from themeList ) customThemes customThemes dict (property is a list of names) {} name \u2192 theme object with colour keys and optional albumArt file URL Saved from MediaRoom ( save_custom_theme ), deleted from the Display page User-created themes captured from album art; art files live in theme_art/ colorTransitionMs colorTransitionMs int 1000 0 \u2013 2000, step 50 (Display slider and pinned-card registry agree) Display page Duration of theme colour transitions in ms. Pinnable songLengthTransition songLengthTransition bool false true/false Display page Animate the theme colour transition over the whole song length instead of colorTransitionMs showClock showClock bool true true/false Display page (Clock); Display widget Show the clock in the bottom bar. Pinnable clockFormat24Hour clockFormat24Hour bool true true/false Display page (Clock) 24-hour clock (false = 12-hour). Signal is clockFormatChanged clockShowSeconds clockShowSeconds bool false true/false Display page (Clock) Show seconds in the bottom-bar clock clockSize clockSize int 18 \u2013 (UI 10 \u2013 85, step 1) Display page (Clock) Clock font size in points bottomBarOrientation bottomBarOrientation str \"bottom\" \"bottom\", \"side\" (UI chips; slot does not validate) Display page (Layout) Navigation bar along the bottom edge or the side showBottomBarMediaControls showBottomBarMediaControls bool true true/false Display page (Layout) Play/skip controls inside the navigation bar. Pinnable settingsLayoutStyle settingsLayoutStyle str \"Sidebar\" \"Carousel\", \"Sidebar\", \"Hub\", \"Dashboard\" (others ignored) Display page (Layout) Which Setti"}, {"url": "settings-ui.html", "title": "Settings UI", "subtitle": "The SettingsMenu orchestrator, the six settings pages and their tile grids, the four layout engines (Sidebar default), the Now Playing studio, the reusable component library, widgets, persistence helpers, and how to add a page", "headings": [{"id": "orchestrator", "text": "SettingsMenu Orchestrator", "level": 2}, {"id": "page-model", "text": "Page Model", "level": 2}, {"id": "pages", "text": "The Settings Pages", "level": 2}, {"id": "tile-system", "text": "Tile Grids and the Card Popup", "level": 2}, {"id": "now-playing-studio", "text": "Now Playing Studio", "level": 2}, {"id": "layouts", "text": "Layout Engines", "level": 2}, {"id": "sidebar", "text": "Sidebar Layout (default)", "level": 2}, {"id": "carousel", "text": "Carousel Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "hub", "text": "Hub Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboard", "text": "Dashboard Layout", "level": 2}, {"id": "components", "text": "Reusable Components", "level": 2}, {"id": "widgets", "text": "Widget System", "level": 2}, {"id": "visibility", "text": "Section Visibility", "level": 2}, {"id": "persistence", "text": "Card State and Scroll Memory", "level": 2}, {"id": "adding-page", "text": "Adding a New Settings Page", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Settings UI - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Settings UI Settings UI The SettingsMenu orchestrator, the six settings pages and their tile grids, the four layout engines (Sidebar default), the Now Playing studio, the reusable component library, widgets, persistence helpers, and how to add a page SettingsMenu Orchestrator frontend/SettingsMenu.qml is pushed onto the StackView by the bottom bar's Settings button with initialSection set to the last visited section. It renders no settings itself; it owns the page model, the visible-page model, the navigation API, and a Loader that instantiates one of four layout engines: Item { id : settingsMenu objectName : \"settingsMenu\" required property var stackView required property var mainWindow required property string initialSection property string currentSection: \"\" property bool updateAvailable: networkManager && networkManager.updateStatus === \"update-available\" property string layoutStyle: settingsManager ? settingsManager.settingsLayoutStyle : \"Sidebar\" property bool isInitialLoad: true } function layoutSourceForStyle (style) { switch (style) { case \"Carousel\" : return \"SettingsCarouselLayout.qml\" case \"Sidebar\" : return \"SettingsSidebarLayout.qml\" case \"Hub\" : return \"SettingsHubLayout.qml\" case \"Dashboard\" : return \"SettingsDashboardLayout.qml\" default : return \"SettingsSidebarLayout.qml\" } } In Component.onCompleted it pre-loads the card-state cache ( ExpandedCards.load(settingsManager) ), builds hubModel , and adopts initialSection if it is a visible section. The layout Loader 's onLoaded hands the layout a reference to the orchestrator and a live binding to hubModel ; for the Carousel it also sets the initial currentIndex . Navigation API navigateToCategory(section) \u2014 sets currentSection , persists it with settingsManager.set_last_settings_section() , then either sets the Carousel's currentIndex or calls the layout's own navigateToCategory() . navigateToSubSection(section, sub) \u2014 delegates to the layout if it implements it (Dashboard does), otherwise falls back to the category. navigateToHub() \u2014 called by the bottom bar when Settings is tapped while already open; forwards to the layout (Hub and Dashboard return to their card grid). A Connections on settingsLayoutStyleChanged sets isInitialLoad = false and swaps the layout; the new engine then keeps currentSection instead of restoring the saved one. A 16 ms polling Timer ( scrollToSectionTimer ) scrolls the Carousel's current page to a card found by findChildByObjectName() when pendingScrollTarget is set. Page Model pageModel is the single source of truth for the six pages: readonly property var pageModel: [ { name : \"Display\" , section : \"displaySettings\" , source : \"settings/DisplaySettingsPage.qml\" , widget : \"widgets/DisplayWidget.qml\" , icon : \"\u263c\" , iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_display.svg\" , group : \"Appearance\" , subSections : [ \"Layout\" , \"Window\" , \"Appearance\" , \"Clock\" ] }, { name : \"Media\" , section : \"mediaSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"media_button.svg\" , group : \"Appearance\" , subSections : [ \"Library\" , \"Playback\" , \"Now Playing\" , \"Spotify\" ] }, { name : \"OBD\" , section : \"obdSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"obd_button.svg\" , group : \"Connectivity\" , subSections : [ \"Connection\" , \"Adapters\" , \"Tuning\" , \"Parameters\" ] }, { name : \"Accessories\" , section : \"accessoriesSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"sensor_button.svg\" , group : \"Connectivity\" , desktopOnly : true , subSections : [ \"Volume Knob\" , \"IMU Sensor\" , \"Gesture Sensor\" , \"Phone Dock\" ] }, { name : \"Device\" , section : \"deviceSettings\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"settings_button.svg\" , group : \"System\" , subSections : [ \"Device Name\" , \"Network\" , \"Power\" ] }, { name : \"About\" , section : \"about\" , ... iconSource : Style.assetBase + \"cat_about.svg\" , group : \"System\" , subSections : [] } ] Key Purpose name Label in tabs, sidebar rows and cards section Identifier used for navigation, persistence ( lastSettingsSection ) and the visibility map source Page QML, relative to frontend/ (loaded by layouts in that directory; SettingsHubCard prepends \"../\" ) widget Dashboard widget QML, relative to frontend/settings/ icon Legacy Unicode glyph, kept only as a fallback iconSource Theme-tinted SVG built from Style.assetBase ; this is what renders group Appearance / Connectivity / System (carried in hubModel , currently informational) desktopOnly buildHubModel() drops the page when the isAndroid context property is true (Accessories) subSections Anchor names for sub-navigation. Must equal the page's tileModel titles : lookup is by objectName , which pages set from modelData.title . The Settings Pages Five of the six pages are Flickable s that declare a tileModel \u2014 an array of { cardId, title, iconSource, component } \u2014 and put each card's body in its own Component . The same components serve two renderings: the page's own stacked SettingsCard list, and the tile grid + popup that the Sidebar "}, {"url": "signals-slots-reference.html", "title": "Signals &amp; Slots Reference", "subtitle": "Exhaustive public API of every backend object registered as a QML context property, verified against both the C++ (src/managers/) and Python (backend/) backends.", "headings": [{"id": "registry", "text": "Context property registry", "level": 2}, {"id": "conventions", "text": "Reading this page", "level": 2}, {"id": "settingsManager", "text": "SettingsManager &mdash; settingsManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "clock", "text": "Clock &mdash; clock", "level": 2}, {"id": "diagnosticsManager", "text": "DiagnosticsManager &mdash; diagnosticsManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "networkManager", "text": "NetworkManager &mdash; networkManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "volumeController", "text": "VolumeController &mdash; volumeController", "level": 2}, {"id": "mediaManager", "text": "MediaManager &mdash; mediaManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "audioAnalyzer", "text": "AudioAnalyzer &mdash; audioAnalyzer", "level": 2}, {"id": "spotifyManager", "text": "SpotifyManager &mdash; spotifyManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "obdManager", "text": "OBDManager &mdash; obdManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "esp32VolumeManager", "text": "ESP32VolumeManager &mdash; esp32VolumeManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "berryIMU", "text": "BerryIMUManager &mdash; berryIMU", "level": 2}, {"id": "gestureSensor", "text": "GestureManager &mdash; gestureSensor", "level": 2}, {"id": "androidAutoManager", "text": "AndroidAutoManager &mdash; androidAutoManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "dhuCapture", "text": "DhuCapture &mdash; dhuCapture", "level": 2}, {"id": "phoneMirrorManager", "text": "PhoneMirrorManager &mdash; phoneMirrorManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "downloadManager", "text": "DownloadManager &mdash; downloadManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "dashboardManager", "text": "DashboardManager &mdash; dashboardManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "perfMonitor", "text": "PerfMonitor &mdash; perfMonitor", "level": 2}, {"id": "parity-gaps", "text": "Known parity gaps", "level": 2}], "body": "Signals & Slots Reference - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Signals & Slots Reference Signals & Slots Reference Exhaustive public API of every backend object registered as a QML context property, verified against both the C++ ( src/managers/ ) and Python ( backend/ ) backends. How managers reach QML Every manager is a QObject registered in src/main.cpp ( ctx->setContextProperty(...) ) and main.py ( engine.rootContext().setContextProperty(...) ) under the same name, so QML never knows which backend is running: mediaManager.next_track() , settingsManager.themeSetting , obdManager.onRpmChanged . Two scalar context properties exist as well: screenAutoScale (float, short screen edge / 720 clamped to 0.4\u20133.0) and isAndroid (bool, always false on Python). The qmlRegisterType 'd OCTAVE.AndroidAuto.EmbeddedDhuItem and the QML singletons ( OBDParameterModel , WidgetCatalog , \u2026) are documented in Components , not here. Context property registry 16 context properties (plus one sub-object and one profiler) \u2014 828 public members in total. QML name C++ class & file Python class & file Notes settingsManager SettingsManager \u2014 src/managers/settingsmanager.h SettingsManager \u2014 backend/settings_manager.py clock Clock \u2014 src/managers/clock.h Clock \u2014 backend/clock.py diagnosticsManager DiagnosticsManager \u2014 src/managers/diagnosticsmanager.h DiagnosticsManager \u2014 backend/diagnostics_manager.py networkManager NetworkManager \u2014 src/managers/networkmanager.h NetworkManager \u2014 backend/network_manager.py volumeController VolumeController \u2014 src/managers/volumecontroller.h VolumeController \u2014 backend/volume_utils.py mediaManager MediaManager \u2014 src/managers/mediamanager.h MediaManager \u2014 backend/media_manager.py audioAnalyzer AudioAnalyzer \u2014 src/managers/audioanalyzer.h AudioAnalyzer \u2014 backend/audio_analyzer.py spotifyManager SpotifyManager \u2014 src/managers/spotifymanager.h SpotifyManager \u2014 backend/spotify_manager.py C++ mobile builds register a stub (see the section) obdManager OBDManager \u2014 src/managers/obdmanager.h OBDManager \u2014 backend/obd_manager.py Python: registered through install_obd_manager() so dev/ can swap in a MockOBDManager esp32VolumeManager ESP32VolumeManager \u2014 src/managers/esp32volumemanager.h ESP32VolumeManager \u2014 backend/esp32_volume_manager.py C++ mobile builds register a stub (see the section) berryIMU BerryIMUManager \u2014 src/managers/berryimumanager.h BerryIMUManager \u2014 backend/berryimu_manager.py C++ Android build uses a QtSensors implementation gestureSensor GestureManager \u2014 src/managers/gesturemanager.h GestureManager \u2014 backend/gesture_manager.py C++ mobile builds register a stub (see the section) androidAutoManager AndroidAutoManager \u2014 src/managers/androidautomanager.h AndroidAutoManager \u2014 backend/android_auto/manager.py C++ mobile builds register a stub (see the section) dhuCapture DhuCapture \u2014 src/managers/androidautomanager.h DhuCapture \u2014 backend/android_auto/dhu_capture.py Not registered directly; reached via androidAutoManager.dhuCapture phoneMirrorManager PhoneMirrorManager \u2014 src/managers/phonemirrormanager.h PhoneMirrorManager \u2014 backend/phone_mirror/manager.py C++ mobile builds register a stub (see the section) downloadManager DownloadManager \u2014 src/managers/downloadmanager.h DownloadManager \u2014 backend/download_manager.py C++: stub class when OCTAVE_ENABLE_DOWNLOADS=OFF dashboardManager DashboardManager \u2014 src/managers/dashboardmanager.h DashboardManager \u2014 backend/dashboard_manager.py perfMonitor \u2014 PerfMonitor \u2014 backend/perf_monitor.py Python only, and only with python main.py --profile Reading this page Properties are Q_PROPERTY (C++) / Property (Python). Almost all are read-only from QML; call the matching save_* / set* slot to change them. Notify signal is what a QML binding or on<Name>Changed handler listens to. Invokables & Slots are Q_INVOKABLE / public slots (C++) and @Slot (Python). Signatures show C++ types; where the Python declaration differs (arity, types, result) the difference is noted inline in grey. QVariantList / QStringList arrive in QML as JS arrays, QVariantMap as objects. Signals list the payload the QML handler receives. C++ only / Python only mark members that exist in only one backend \u2014 QML must not call them without a typeof guard. Android marks members that only do something on Android. Where the C++ class has a separate mobile ( Q_OS_MOBILE ) stub, the info box at the top of the section lists what the stub lacks. SettingsManager \u2014 settingsManager src/managers/settingsmanager.h / backend/settings_manager.py \u2014 QML context property settingsManager Central settings store. Persists settingsConfigure.json in the OS config dir (writes are debounced; flushPendingSave() forces them). Every setting follows the same triple: a read-only Q_PROPERTY , a <name>Changed signal and a save_<snake_name>() slot. Types and defaults for every key are in the Settings Reference . Properties Property Type Access Notify signal Description deviceName QString read-only deviceNameChanged Name shown on the home screen / About page \u2014 "}, {"url": "spotify-manager.html", "title": "Spotify Manager", "subtitle": "Spotify Connect remote control: OAuth2 Authorization Code flow, adaptive playback polling, and album-art theming on both backends", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "dependencies", "text": "Dependencies", "level": 2}, {"id": "credential-setup", "text": "Credential Setup", "level": 2}, {"id": "oauth-flow", "text": "OAuth2 Authentication Flow", "level": 2}, {"id": "keyring-storage", "text": "Token Storage", "level": 2}, {"id": "async-pattern", "text": "Async API Call Pattern", "level": 2}, {"id": "playback-control", "text": "Playback Control", "level": 2}, {"id": "polling", "text": "Playback State Polling", "level": 2}, {"id": "position-interpolation", "text": "Position Interpolation", "level": 2}, {"id": "device-management", "text": "Device Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "playlist-management", "text": "Playlist Management", "level": 2}, {"id": "queue-and-sidecards", "text": "Queue and Side Cards", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-theming", "text": "Album Art Theming", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume", "text": "Volume Routing", "level": 2}, {"id": "disconnect-cleanup", "text": "Disconnect and Cleanup", "level": 2}, {"id": "error-handling", "text": "Error Handling", "level": 2}, {"id": "qml-consumers", "text": "QML Consumers", "level": 2}, {"id": "signals", "text": "Key Signals", "level": 2}, {"id": "slots", "text": "Key Slots", "level": 2}, {"id": "backend-differences", "text": "C++ vs Python Differences", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Spotify Manager - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Spotify Manager Spotify Manager Spotify Connect remote control: OAuth2 Authorization Code flow, adaptive playback polling, and album-art theming on both backends Overview The SpotifyManager class ( src/managers/spotifymanager.{h,cpp} in C++, backend/spotify_manager.py in Python) controls Spotify playback on connected devices (phone, desktop, smart speaker, etc.) via the Spotify Web API. It mirrors the MediaManager signal interface ( playStateChanged , currentTrackChanged , durationChanged , positionChanged , \u2026) so QML can treat local and Spotify playback through one UI. It is registered as the QML context property spotifyManager in src/main.cpp / main.py . SpotifyManager does not play audio -- it is a remote control for Spotify Connect devices. It sends play/pause/skip/seek/volume/shuffle commands over the API and polls GET /v1/me/player for state (every 3 seconds while playing, 5 seconds while paused, 10 seconds when no device is active). Between polls a local 250 ms timer interpolates the progress position so the slider moves smoothly without extra API traffic. Spotify is desktop-only . The C++ header wraps the real class in #ifndef Q_OS_MOBILE ; the mobile build compiles a stub with the same signals and slots so QML bindings stay valid ( is_connected() always returns false ). The header comment gives the reason: no OAuth URI-scheme redirect handler is wired on mobile, and the project scope keeps Spotify on desktop. The Python backend is desktop-only by policy (see Architecture ). Desktop Dependencies Backend Package Purpose Required? Python spotipy Spotify Web API wrapper and SpotifyOAuth token management Yes -- authenticate() refuses to run without it; is_available() reports the flag Python keyring OS keychain storage for the OAuth token Optional -- falls back to a file cache Python numpy k-means colour clustering for Album Art Capture Only when that theme is active (imported inside the worker) C++ Qt Network ( QNetworkAccessManager , QTcpServer ) REST calls, token exchange, OAuth redirect listener, album-art download Always linked; is_available() returns true The Python backend does not import spotipy or keyring at startup. Both are probed with available() (a find_spec check that performs no import) and bound through lazy_module() , a proxy that imports on first attribute access ( backend/lazy_import.py ). The module comment records why: spotipy pulls in redis , and together with keyring the imports cost about 0.4 s on the Raspberry Pi for a feature that is only needed once the user connects. # spotipy (which imports redis) and keyring together cost ~0.4 s at import on # the Pi and are only needed once the user connects; import them on first use. SPOTIPY_AVAILABLE = available( \"spotipy\" ) if not SPOTIPY_AVAILABLE: logger.warning( \"spotipy not installed. Run: pip install spotipy\" ) spotipy = lazy_module( \"spotipy\" ) KEYRING_AVAILABLE = available( \"keyring\" ) if not KEYRING_AVAILABLE: logger.warning( \"keyring not installed. Token storage will be less secure.\" ) keyring = lazy_module( \"keyring\" ) Credential Setup Users must provide a Spotify Developer application's Client ID and Client Secret. The Spotify card on frontend/settings/MediaSettingsPage.qml writes them with settingsManager.save_spotify_credentials(id, secret) ; SettingsManager persists them as the spotifyClientId / spotifyClientSecret keys of settingsConfigure.json and emits spotifyCredentialsChanged . Spotify's Development Mode rules (February 2026). OCTAVE is a personal app, so it runs as a Development Mode app registered by the user. Since Spotify's February 2026 migration (new apps from 11 February, existing apps from 9 March 2026) that means: the app owner must hold an active Spotify Premium subscription (the app stops working if it lapses), one Client ID per developer, and at most five allow-listed users per app. Everything OCTAVE needs survived the migration: the player endpoints ( /me/player state, play, pause, next, previous, seek, volume, shuffle, queue, devices, transfer) and /me/playlists are unchanged; playlist contents moved from /playlists/{id}/tracks to /playlists/{id}/items (50 per page, track under item ) and are only readable for playlists the user owns or collaborates on, so a followed editorial playlist lists but will not load its tracks. OCTAVE never used the removed browse, recommendations, audio-features or other-user endpoints. Both backends read the credentials once, when the settings manager is wired ( SpotifyManager::setSettingsManager() in C++, connect_settings_manager() in Python): void SpotifyManager :: loadCredentials () { if (!m_settingsManager) return ; m_clientId = m_settingsManager->get_spotify_client_id(); m_clientSecret = m_settingsManager->get_spotify_client_secret(); } The Python backend additionally exposes set_credentials(client_id, client_secret) , which updates its own copy and calls save_spotify_credentials() ; the C++ backend has no such slot and QML does"}, {"url": "theme-system.html", "title": "Theme System", "subtitle": "Style.qml colour themes and fonts, Album Art Capture (k-means palettes from cover art in both backends), colour transitions, the single-preset EnvironmentTheme, Spacing.qml layout scaling and the GUI scale slider, and the ThemedIcon SVG tinting pipeline", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview", "level": 2}, {"id": "style-qml", "text": "Style.qml &mdash; Theme Resolution", "level": 2}, {"id": "builtin-themes", "text": "Built-in Themes", "level": 2}, {"id": "theme-object", "text": "Theme Object Structure", "level": 2}, {"id": "theme-picker", "text": "Theme Picker and Swatch Chips", "level": 2}, {"id": "custom-themes", "text": "Custom (Saved Album Art) Themes", "level": 2}, {"id": "album-art-capture", "text": "Album Art Capture", "level": 2}, {"id": "color-tokens", "text": "Colour Token Reference", "level": 2}, {"id": "transitions", "text": "Colour Transitions", "level": 2}, {"id": "font-system", "text": "Font System", "level": 2}, {"id": "environment-theme", "text": "EnvironmentTheme.qml &mdash; Structural Tokens", "level": 2}, {"id": "spacing-qml", "text": "Spacing.qml &mdash; Layout Scaling", "level": 2}, {"id": "scaling-functions", "text": "Scaling Functions", "level": 2}, {"id": "gui-scale", "text": "GUI Scale Slider and Auto-Scale", "level": 2}, {"id": "themed-icon", "text": "ThemedIcon and the SVG Assets", "level": 2}, {"id": "consuming-themes", "text": "How Components Consume the Theme", "level": 2}, {"id": "customization", "text": "Customization Guide", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design Decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Theme System - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Theme System Theme System Style.qml colour themes and fonts, Album Art Capture (k-means palettes from cover art in both backends), colour transitions, the single-preset EnvironmentTheme, Spacing.qml layout scaling and the GUI scale slider, and the ThemedIcon SVG tinting pipeline Overview OCTAVE's look is driven by three pragma Singleton QML objects declared in frontend/qmldir and imported everywhere as App.* ( import \".\" as App from frontend/ , import \"..\" as App from frontend/settings/ ). Files that live in frontend/ itself can also reference them unqualified ( SettingsMenu.qml writes Style.assetBase ). +----------------------+ +------------------------+ +----------------------+ | Style.qml | | EnvironmentTheme.qml | | Spacing.qml | | | | | | | | COLOURS + FONTS | | STRUCTURE (one preset) | | SIZES | | - 17 built-in themes | | - cardRadius, chip / | | - dp(), dpMin() | | - custom themes | | button / switch radii| | - scaledSize() | | - Album Art Capture | | - feature flags, all | | - scalePx() | | - ~60 colour tokens, | | false in \"Standard\" | | - percentage tokens | | each with Behavior | | | | for every screen | | - fontFamily, fonts | | | | - globalScale / auto | | - assetBase URL | | | | scale factors | +----------------------+ +------------------------+ +----------------------+ | | | +--------------------------+----------------------------+ | Wired at startup by frontend/Main.qml from settingsManager (themeSetting, fontSetting, uiScale, colorTransitionMs, ...) The backends own persistence only. SettingsManager (both C++ and Python) stores themeSetting , fontSetting , uiScale , colorTransitionMs , songLengthTransition , customThemes and albumArtColors ; MediaManager and SpotifyManager produce Album Art Capture palettes. Everything else is QML. Style.qml \u2014 Theme Resolution frontend/Style.qml (about 1200 lines) holds the built-in palettes, the custom-theme map, the live album-art palette, the font registry, and one reactive colour property per UI token. The activeTheme object is what every token binds to, resolved in priority order: property var activeTheme: { // 1. Album Art Capture with a live palette if (currentTheme === \"Album Art Capture\" && albumArtTheme) return albumArtTheme // 2. Custom themes saved by the user if (customThemes[currentTheme]) return customThemes[currentTheme] // 3. Built-in themes, with a hard fallback return themes[currentTheme] || themes[ \"SolarizedLight\" ] } Two setters exist. setTheme(name) validates the name against themes and customThemes , records lastStaticTheme (unless the name is Album Art Capture, in which case it arms _firstAlbumArtUpdate ) and assigns currentTheme , letting the Behavior s animate. setThemeInstant(name) does the same but zeroes colorTransitionMs for the duration of the switch and restores it with Qt.callLater . Main.qml uses setThemeInstant for the startup load and for every manual theme change; the Display page's chips call setTheme after persisting through settingsManager.save_theme_setting() . Other public members: getAllThemeNames() (built-ins minus \"Album Art Capture\", plus custom names), getLightThemeNames() / getDarkThemeNames() , addCustomTheme(name, obj) (emits customThemesUpdated ), updateAlbumArtTheme(json) , isAlbumArtCaptureActive , lastStaticTheme , and the read-only URL assetBase ( Qt.resolvedUrl(\"assets/\") ). Built-in Themes Seventeen palettes are defined in the themes map: eight light, eight dark, plus the \"Album Art Capture\" placeholder. The picker order below is hand-tuned in lightThemeNames / darkThemeNames (\"so the swatch palette flows nicely\"). The default on a fresh install is CosmicVoyager in both backends ( themeSetting default in src/managers/settingsmanager.cpp and backend/settings_manager.py ). Light Theme base baseAlt accent text.primary SolarizedLight #FDF6E3 #EEE8D5 #268BD2 #073642 ArcticChill #F0F4F8 #DCE5EE #0EA5E9 #0F2942 Parchment #F5EBD8 #E8DBC1 #8B4513 #2A1A0A Sunshine #FFFCF2 #FFF6D8 #F97316 #3A1E03 MintGarden #F0F9F4 #DDF0E2 #16A34A #0F2A1A RoseQuartz #FBF2F4 #F5E0E5 #BE185D #3A0E1F LavenderMist #F5F3FA #E9E4F2 #6D28D9 #1F0E3D SlateCloud #F1F5F9 #E2E8F0 #475569 #0F172A Dark Theme base baseAlt accent text.primary AmberConsole #000000 #0A0A0A #FFB000 #FFE89C NeonMatrix #0C0C0C #161616 #00FF41 #CCFFCC ForestEmerald #0A1A14 #122620 #10B981 #D1FAE5 CosmicVoyager (default) #0A0E17 #151C29 #00BFFF #E0F2FF TechnoRetro #1E1014 #2A1B22 #FF00AA #F5E0FF QuantumNebula #0F0F1A #17172A #9C27B0 #F0E6FF CrimsonEmber #1A0808 #241010 #DC2626 #FFE4E4 AutumnCascade #2D1E12 #3E2918 #E67E22 #FDF2E9 Placeholder Theme base baseAlt accent Note Album Art Capture #1a1a2e #16213e #e94560 Only used when no palette has been extracted yet; excluded from getAllThemeNames() and surfaced as a toggle, not a chip. Themes differ in how much per-button variety they carry. The eight themes added with the swatch redesign (commit 7ac3c31 : ArcticChill, Parchment, Sunshine, MintGarden, RoseQuartz, LavenderMist, Sla"}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "subtitle": "Symptom \u2192 cause \u2192 fix for both backends, where the logs are and what is in them, and the observability built into the app for diagnosing problems after the fact.", "headings": [{"id": "first-look", "text": "Where to Look First", "level": 2}, {"id": "logs", "text": "Log Files, Crash Traces &amp; Debug Mode", "level": 2}, {"id": "installation", "text": "Installation Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "display", "text": "Display &amp; Rendering Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "audio", "text": "Audio Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "obd", "text": "OBD Connection Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "android-obd", "text": "Android OBD (Bluetooth / BLE)", "level": 2}, {"id": "spotify", "text": "Spotify Authentication Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "downloads", "text": "Music Download (yt-dlp) Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "phone-mirror", "text": "Phone Mirror Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "hardware-sensors", "text": "Hardware Sensor Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "performance", "text": "Performance Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "dev-tooling", "text": "Dev Tooling Issues", "level": 2}, {"id": "wsl", "text": "WSL Display Issues", "level": 2}], "body": "Troubleshooting - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Troubleshooting Troubleshooting Symptom \u2192 cause \u2192 fix for both backends, where the logs are and what is in them, and the observability built into the app for diagnosing problems after the fact. Where to Look First OCTAVE deliberately has no in-app error pop-ups ( TODO/error-notification-ui.md : anything flickering into a driver's peripheral vision is a distraction risk, so errors go to the logs where the person debugging will look). Every subsystem therefore reports through three channels: The Connection Log / status text on the relevant page \u2014 the OBD settings card shows each connection step and rfcomm / permission errors; the Download page shows Error: <name> - <message> and marks the queue row failed ; the phone-mirror view shows the adb / authorization message. Settings \u2192 About \u2192 Diagnostics card ( diagnosticsManager , both backends) \u2014 a 2\u00d72 grid: Refresh (last 200 log lines), Copy (last 400 lines to the clipboard), Export logs (copies the log files to the Downloads folder \u2014 the way to get logs off an Android tablet or a head unit without a shell) and Open folder (hidden on Android/iOS, where canOpenFolder is false). The log directory path is printed on the card. The rotating log files described next. A hung UI, a crash and a dropped adapter all leave a trace there even when the screen shows nothing. Log Files, Crash Traces & Debug Mode Log directory Both backends write to a logs/ subdirectory of the app data directory ( SettingsManager::getAppDataDir() / get_app_data_dir() ): Platform Path Windows %APPDATA%\\OCTAVE\\logs\\ macOS ~/Library/Application Support/OCTAVE/logs/ Linux $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/OCTAVE/logs/ , i.e. ~/.config/OCTAVE/logs/ Android getAppDataDir() has no Android branch, so the C++ build falls through the Linux rule into the app's private home directory. It is not browsable from a file manager \u2014 use Export logs on the Diagnostics card, which writes into Download/ . On Android and iOS the message handler also forwards every line to Qt's default handler (stderr goes nowhere on a phone), so adb logcat / os_log show the same messages. Log files \u2014 the two backends use different names Backend File Level Max size Backups Notes Python octave.log INFO+ 5 MB 3 backend/logging_config.py , RotatingFileHandler Python octave-error.log ERROR+ 2 MB 5 also receives crash tracebacks and faulthandler dumps Python octave-debug.log DEBUG+ 10 MB 2 only with --debug C++ octave-cpp.log INFO+ 5 MB 3 src/util/logger.cpp , custom rotation with the same policy C++ octave-cpp-error.log WARNING+ 2 MB 5 also the raw crash descriptor for the signal handler C++ octave-cpp-debug.log everything 10 MB 2 only with --debug Because the names differ, a machine that has run both backends has both sets side by side; check the timestamps to see which one produced the session you care about. Total disk is bounded at roughly 32 MB per backend without debug, ~62 MB with it. Line format # Python console (HH:MM:SS): HH:MM:SS | LEVEL | octave.module | message # Python file: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS | LEVEL | octave.module | file.py:line | function() | message # C++ file and stderr (same shape, millisecond timestamps, Qt category in the module column): YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.zzz | LEVEL | octave.elm327 | obdmanager.cpp:1772 | message Python logger names come from get_logger(__name__) : octave.obd , octave.media , octave.spotify , octave.settings , octave.clock , octave.android_auto[.capture|.embedded] , octave.phone_mirror[.host|.capture|.container|.embedded] , octave.app for main.py , and octave.<module> for everything else (e.g. octave.ui_watchdog , octave.elm327_protocol ). python-obd's own obd logger tree is attached to the same handlers so an ELM327 session leaves its write: / read: transcript in octave-debug.log . The C++ side uses one QLoggingCategory per manager: octave.settings , octave.media , octave.spotify , octave.download , octave.network , octave.phonemirror (+ octave.phonemirror.client for the scrcpy-protocol client), octave.androidauto , octave.embeddeddhu , octave.audioanalyzer , octave.berryimu , octave.gesture , octave.esp32volume , octave.dashboards , octave.diagnostics , octave.ui_watchdog and octave.elm327 (protocol transcript and the Android BLE bridge lines). The exceptions are obdmanager.cpp , clock.cpp and volumecontroller.cpp , which log through plain qDebug() / qWarning() and therefore appear under the category default \u2014 so there is no octave.obd category to filter on in the C++ logs; look for the [OBD Worker] message prefix instead. With --debug the C++ build sets the filter rules *.debug=true , qt.*.debug=false ; without it *.debug=false , so category-debug output costs nothing. Crash traces Python: sys.excepthook and threading.excepthook log Unhandled exception on the main thread / in thread '<name>' with the traceback at CRITICAL, and faulthandler is enabled on a descriptor of octave-error.log so a native fault (SIGSEGV, SIGABRT, SIGFPE, SIGBUS, SIGILL \u2014 e.g. inside PySide6 o"}, {"url": "utilities.html", "title": "Utility Modules", "subtitle": "Every supporting module that is not a feature manager, in both backends: Clock, logging and crash handlers, the UI watchdog, DiagnosticsManager, VolumeController, version, lazy imports, the performance monitor, the command server, the C++ src/util / src/platform trees, and the backend/media / backend/temp directories.", "headings": [{"id": "overview", "text": "Overview &amp; file map", "level": 2}, {"id": "clock", "text": "Clock", "level": 2}, {"id": "logging", "text": "Logging", "level": 2}, {"id": "crash-handlers", "text": "Crash handlers &amp; signal shutdown", "level": 2}, {"id": "ui-watchdog", "text": "UI watchdog", "level": 2}, {"id": "diagnostics-manager", "text": "DiagnosticsManager", "level": 2}, {"id": "volume-controller", "text": "VolumeController", "level": 2}, {"id": "version", "text": "Version &amp; update check", "level": 2}, {"id": "lazy-import", "text": "Lazy imports (Python)", "level": 2}, {"id": "perf-monitor", "text": "Performance monitor &amp; perf tooling (Python)", "level": 2}, {"id": "command-server", "text": "Command server (Python)", "level": 2}, {"id": "platform", "text": "C++ src/util/ and src/platform/", "level": 2}, {"id": "backend-dirs", "text": "backend/media and backend/temp", "level": 2}, {"id": "design-decisions", "text": "Design decisions", "level": 2}], "body": "Utility Modules - OCTAVE Wiki OCTAVE Wiki / Utility Modules Utility Modules Every supporting module that is not a feature manager, in both backends: Clock, logging and crash handlers, the UI watchdog, DiagnosticsManager, VolumeController, version, lazy imports, the performance monitor, the command server, the C++ src/util / src/platform trees, and the backend/media / backend/temp directories. Overview & file map The feature managers (media, Spotify, OBD, sensors, phone mirror, downloads, dashboards) each have their own wiki page. This page covers everything else that supports them. The table shows where each piece lives in each backend, whether it is a QObject that QML can reach, and the context-property name registered in src/main.cpp / main.py . Module C++ Python QML context property Notes Clock src/managers/clock.{h,cpp} backend/clock.py clock Both backends Logging src/util/logger.{h,cpp} ( OctaveLog namespace) backend/logging_config.py \u2013 Not a QObject; installed first thing in main() Crash handlers inside src/util/logger.cpp logging_config._install_crash_handlers() \u2013 Write to the error log UI watchdog src/util/uiwatchdog.{h,cpp} backend/ui_watchdog.py \u2013 (not registered) QObject, but internal only DiagnosticsManager src/managers/diagnosticsmanager.{h,cpp} backend/diagnostics_manager.py diagnosticsManager Settings \u2192 About \u2192 Diagnostics card VolumeController src/managers/volumecontroller.{h,cpp} backend/volume_utils.py volumeController Quadratic curve + dispatch Version project(OCTAVE VERSION \u2026) in CMakeLists.txt \u2192 OCTAVE_VERSION define backend/version.py \u2013 (surfaced as diagnosticsManager.appVersion ) Kept in lockstep by hand Lazy imports \u2013 backend/lazy_import.py \u2013 Python-only Performance monitor \u2013 backend/perf_monitor.py , backend/perf_patches.py perfMonitor (only with --profile ) Python-only, gitignored Command server \u2013 backend/command_server.py \u2013 Python-only, gitignored, only with --profile Android JNI bridges src/platform/androidmediabridge.h , src/platform/androidprocessadapter.h \u2013 \u2013 C++-only (mobile is C++-only by policy) Dev-only files that are not in a fresh clone .gitignore lists backend/perf_monitor.py , backend/perf_patches.py and backend/command_server.py under \u201cPerformance profiling (lives in dev/perf/)\u201d, together with the whole dev/ tree, backend/temp/ and .mcp.json . They exist on the developer machines but are not versioned. main.py only imports them inside setup_perf_profiling() , which runs when --profile is passed, and every one of those imports is guarded: on a checkout without them --profile logs --profile: backend/perf_monitor.py not present (gitignored dev tooling), skipping performance monitor and command server (or the command_server.py equivalent) and carries on. A normal python main.py never touches them. Clock src/managers/clock.{h,cpp} / backend/clock.py \u2014 QML name: clock (both backends) A QTimer-based clock that formats the current time once a second and emits it as a string. It reads three settings from SettingsManager on every tick \u2014 showClock , clockFormat24Hour and clockShowSeconds \u2014 so a settings change takes effect within a second without any signal wiring. Since commit 7777777 it emits timeChanged only when the text actually changed : without seconds the string changes once a minute, so the QML bindings that show the clock re-evaluate 60\u00d7 less often, and a hidden clock emits the empty string exactly once instead of forever. Public API Member Kind C++ Python Description timeChanged(str) signal void timeChanged(const QString &) Signal(str) Formatted time, or \"\" when showClock is off update_time() slot public slots @Slot() Timer callback (1000 ms); computes the text and emits on change constructor \u2013 Clock(SettingsManager *, QObject *parent = nullptr) Clock(settings_manager) Starts the timer immediately Format Setting Without seconds With seconds clockFormat24Hour = true 14:30 14:30:05 clockFormat24Hour = false 02:30 PM 02:30:05 PM showClock = false \"\" (emitted once) Python uses strftime ( %H:%M / %I:%M plus an upper-cased %p ); C++ builds the 12-hour form by hand ( hour % 12 , 0 \u2192 12, zero-padded) because QTime::toString has no locale-independent AM/PM token. The output strings are identical. Emit-on-change (both backends) void Clock :: update_time () { QString text; if (m_settingsManager->showClock()) { // ... build \"HH:mm[:ss]\" or \"hh:mm[:ss] AM/PM\" ... } // Without seconds the text changes once a minute; re-evaluating every // binding that shows the clock 60 times for the same string is waste. if (text == m_lastText) return ; m_lastText = text; emit timeChanged(text); } def _emit_if_changed (self, text): if text == self._last_text: return self._last_text = text self.timeChanged.emit(text) QML consumers frontend/BottomBar.qml \u2014 one Connections { target: clock } at bar level writes a shared clockTimeText property that both the horizontal and the vertical layout bind to, so the layout loaded on an orientation switch shows the current time at once instead of waiting for the"}];
